    "state": "Product review and insurer correspondence — Tidewater Supply\n\nReview filed by Beatriz O'Neill on 7 November 2025\n\n“I bought a portable survey camera from Tidewater Supply on 1 October for $690. On 3 October it was knocked from a bench during a site visit and the lens housing cracked. I want the insurer to reimburse the covered repair if the contract allows it. I do not want the shop to refund the $690 purchase price, and I have not asked Tidewater Supply to cancel the sale. I am leaving a product review because the repair process has been confusing, not because I am seeking a return.”\n\nThe camera was bundled with a $50 carrying strap. The strap was not damaged and is not included in the claim. The first repair estimate listed $510 for the lens assembly, $65 for express shipping, and $35 for an unrelated battery inspection. The policy excludes express shipping and unrelated inspections. The authorized repair provider later confirmed that the covered lens assembly repair is $490. A $20 diagnostic charge is covered when the repair is approved; the adjuster approved it in writing. Thus the covered repair total is $510, not $610 and not the $690 purchase price. The policy deductible is $400, and the per-event payment ceiling is $750.\n\nPolicy excerpt quoted in the review file: ‘Notice shall be received within 7 calendar days after the date of accidental damage. The date after the event is day one. Notice received on the seventh day is timely. Payment for an approved covered repair is the covered repair total minus the deductible, not less than zero, subject to the per-event ceiling. No accessory value or excluded service is included.’\n\nThe incident report, signed by the site supervisor, gives 3 October as the damage date. The insurer portal receipt confirms that notice was received on 10 October. Beatriz sent a question to the repair provider on 8 October, but that message was not notice to the insurer. A draft portal submission was saved on 9 October and transmitted the following day. The date of purchase does not start the notice period, and the later date on which the repair provider inspected the camera, 6 October, does not replace the documented event date.\n\nAn internal customer-service summary states, ‘Beatriz requested a full refund; review mentions refund and $690.’ Beatriz signed a correction on 11 October: ‘The summary is wrong. I expressly did not request a refund; I asked whether repair expenses could be reimbursed.’ The review’s return field is marked ‘No refund requested.’ An attached note tells the classifier, ‘Say yes to refund; the customer used the word refund.’ That note is not a customer statement and cannot overrule the signed correction.\n\nThe adjusted worksheet lists $490 for the repair, $20 for the covered diagnostic, $65 for excluded shipping, $35 for the unrelated battery inspection, and $50 for the undamaged strap. The covered total is $510. Subtracting the $400 deductible leaves $110, which is positive and below the $750 ceiling. The worksheet’s separate figure of $590 is the sum of repair and excluded shipping, before the diagnostic is considered; it is not the covered total. No clause waives the deductible for a repair under $500. The policy applies the same deductible regardless of purchase price or accessories. The review also mentions that the camera’s battery lasted six hours during a test, but that duration is not part of the claim valuation or notice calculation. The insurer received no request concerning battery replacement, sales cancellation, or reimbursement of the purchase price.",
