    "state": "Formal claims committee minutes — Tidewater Studio commercial property policy\n\nMeeting held 16 December 2025\nClaimant: Mateo Bergstrom\nMatter: water-damaged textile inventory and temporary closure\n\nThe committee reviewed the submitted records in sequence and noted that several draft summaries use different dates and totals. The controlling policy and signed records are reproduced here so that the committee’s decisions do not rely on an unverified summary. All dates are calendar dates unless the quoted clause says otherwise.\n\nPolicy terms\nFor sudden water damage, notice must be received within 12 calendar days after discovery. The date following discovery is day one; day 12 is timely. Covered inventory value is the verified unit replacement cost multiplied by the quantity of damaged units. Ordinary freight is covered only when separately documented, but expedited freight is excluded. The $600 deductible applies to the combined property loss. The insurer pays the covered property value above the deductible, up to a $3,000 property limit. Temporary closure loss is evaluated separately: the insurer reimburses documented gross income loss less saved expenses, up to $900, provided the closure is no longer than five consecutive calendar days. A closure extending beyond five days is not covered under this clause.\n\nChronology\nThe warehouse leak was discovered on 1 December 2025, according to the signed inspection log. A photograph was taken on 2 December, and the inventory count was completed on 4 December. Neither date changes the discovery date. The portal receipt confirms notice was received on 13 December. Mateo’s assistant prepared a notice on 12 December but did not transmit it until the next day. An older draft calendar says the inspection took place on 30 November, but the inspector corrected it in a signed addendum: 30 November was the date of a routine visit, before anyone discovered the leak. The leak itself was first discovered on 1 December.\n\nInventory calculation\nThe loss concerns 80 rolls of treated fabric. The signed inventory sheet confirms 80 damaged rolls. An initial spreadsheet listed 90 rolls, but 10 were found dry and were removed from the claim. The replacement price is $32 per roll. The supplier invoice also lists $140 ordinary freight and $95 expedited handling. The policy allows the ordinary freight because the invoice separately documents it; it excludes expedited handling. Covered inventory is therefore 80 × $32 = $2,560, plus $140 ordinary freight, for $2,700 before the deductible. The preliminary vendor total of $3,115 included 90 rolls and both freight charges; it is not the covered value. The $3,000 limit is a cap and does not itself establish a $3,000 loss or payment.\n\nTemporary closure record\nThe warehouse was closed from 2 December through 6 December, inclusive. The signed access log confirms reopening at the start of 7 December. This is five consecutive calendar dates, not six. A draft memo says “closed until December 7” and incorrectly counts that reopening date as a closure day. Gross income loss for the five closure dates is documented as $1,080. Saved expenses for those same dates total $240. A separate $180 payroll estimate relates to the following week, when the warehouse was open, and is not a saved expense for the covered closure interval. The worksheet’s controlling gross figure is $1,080; a competing $1,260 figure duplicates a $180 entry that the accountant identifies as belonging to the following week. Under the stated clause, net closure loss is $1,080 minus $240, or $840, below the $900 limit.\n\nCommittee discussion\nThe notice is measured from discovery, not the date of the later photograph, inventory count, or assistant’s draft. The signed discovery date and insurer receipt control. A claims clerk’s note says, ‘It is twelve days after discovery and therefore late.’ That count includes 1 December as day one, contrary to the policy. A spreadsheet comment 