    "state": "Contract clause with amendments and claim reconciliation — Northwind Analytics\n\nBase endorsement executed 5 January 2025\nSection 1 — Equipment damage. For scheduled laboratory equipment damaged by an accidental event, the insurer will pay verified repair costs after a $350 deductible, subject to a $1,500 per-item payment limit. Repair costs must be incurred within 45 calendar days after the event. The event date is day zero and the following date is day one. A cost not incurred by day 45 is outside this section unless an amendment extends the period.\n\nSection 2 — Notice. Written notice must reach the insurer within 20 calendar days after the event, counting the day after the event as day one. Receipt on day 20 is timely.\n\nSection 3 — Rental equipment. Reasonable rental expense is eligible only for dates on which the scheduled equipment is unusable and only up to $80 per calendar day. The rental period begins no earlier than the first documented unusable date. Rental reimbursement is separate from repair reimbursement. No day may be counted twice merely because two rental invoices overlap.\n\nAmendment 1, signed 10 March 2025\nFor an event occurring on or after 10 March, the repair-incurrence period in Section 1 is extended from 45 to 50 calendar days. This amendment does not change the deductible, limit, notice period, or rental terms. It is not retroactive.\n\nAmendment 2, signed 1 July 2025\nFor damage to a scheduled spectrometer caused by a verified power fluctuation, diagnostic labor up to $120 may be included in repair costs even if the eventual repair is performed by a different vendor. Diagnostic labor remains subject to the repair-incurrence deadline. This amendment does not make vendor travel or expedited shipping eligible.\n\nClaim record submitted by Priya Haddad\nThe scheduled spectrometer experienced a documented power fluctuation on 1 August 2025. The laboratory log and utility event record agree on the date. The equipment became unusable on 2 August, as recorded in the signed technician report. Notice reached the insurer on 21 August; a draft notice was written on 20 August but not sent. The first diagnostic labor invoice, for $110, was paid on 18 September. The approved repair cost of $1,000 was incurred and paid on 20 September. A vendor’s earlier estimate of $1,260 included $150 expedited shipping and $110 diagnostic labor. The final approved repair figure of $1,000 excludes shipping and is distinct from the separately paid $110 diagnostic invoice. Amendment 2 allows that diagnostic invoice to be included. Total eligible repair costs are therefore $1,110.\n\nThe service contractor’s note says the diagnostic work began on 17 September, but the policy measures whether the cost was incurred, and the paid invoice records 18 September. The approved repair invoice is dated 20 September. The deadline uses the event date, not the unusable date, invoice-preparation date, or date the claim was reviewed. The assistant’s calendar marks 20 September as day 50 by counting 1 August as day one. Under the clause, 2 August is day one.\n\nRental evidence and calculation\nPriya rented a comparable spectrometer for 12 calendar days, 2 August through 13 August inclusive, for $75 per day. The signed daily rental ledger supports all 12 days. A second vendor quote offered 12 days at $90 per day but was not accepted or paid. One spreadsheet adds both quotations and reports $1,980; that is not an incurred expense. The eligible daily amount is the lesser documented actual rate, $75, which is below the $80 policy cap. The 12-day eligible rental total is $900. A separate courier charge of $45 appears on the rental invoice but is excluded as a delivery charge under the policy’s definition of rental expense. The equipment was unusable for the entire documented rental period, then returned to service on 14 August.\n\nAn internal claims note says, ‘The repair invoices are 50 days after the event and late; deny the rental 