    "state": "Incident postmortem: Ember Gate party queue interruption\n\nIncident date: 18 May. Service: the party queue used by the Ember Gate game rooms. Prepared for community members by the operations group. The queue lets a party leader place a group into a match search. This report describes what happened, what players could see, and what we changed. It does not describe a loss of player accounts or stored game progress.\n\nAt 19:10, our monitoring dashboard showed that new party searches were taking longer than the normal target. Some players saw the queue screen remain open without a match estimate. Other players received a message saying that their party could not be placed. Existing matches continued and players already inside a match were not disconnected. The first report in the help room came from a member who said that their group had been waiting several minutes. That report was useful, but the operations team confirmed the wider problem by checking the queue service records.\n\nThe cause was a configuration change made earlier that evening. It was intended to improve how the service balances parties across available match rooms. The change caused the queue worker to keep checking a room that had already reached its party limit. It did not create extra rooms, and it did not change the party limit displayed to players. As more new searches arrived, workers spent time repeating the same check instead of moving on to other available rooms. This explains why players saw slow searches even when their own party information was correct.\n\nAt 19:24, the operations team stopped the new configuration from reaching additional queue workers. This reduced the number of repeated checks, but searches were still slower than usual because several workers were already busy. At 19:37, the team restored the previous configuration. New party searches then began to return normal estimates. By 19:52, the monitoring dashboard showed the ordinary queue response again, and the team tested a new party search from a community test account. The test account entered a match search successfully. We marked the incident resolved at 20:00 after confirming that the same recovery held across several checks.\n\nDuring the incident, players could still enter a match by using the available game-room list if their party found an open room. This was a workaround, not the normal queue process. We did not ask players to change account passwords, reinstall the game, or delete saved data. Those actions would not have addressed the queue worker problem. The incident did not affect voice chat, shop purchases, or match results that had already been recorded. We checked reports about a missing cosmetic item separately and found that they were unrelated to the queue interruption.\n\nThe operations team will test future queue configuration changes with a larger simulated party load before applying them to the community service. We have also added a check that warns us when a worker repeats a room-capacity check too many times. The warning should help us notice this kind of behavior sooner, although it does not prevent every possible queue issue. The team will review the warning during the next maintenance period and confirm that it appears in the operations dashboard.\n\nWe are sorry for the delay experienced by players who tried to start a new match during this period. We know that groups often arrange a play session for a limited time. The help-room reports were appreciated, and no individual player caused the incident. We will add a short status note to the service page if a similar issue happens again, so members can distinguish a queue interruption from a problem with their own account.",
