iew charge. Quill’s response = deny adjustment after audit. Supplier relations = not involved.” The ledger’s “vendor” label means the issuer of this invoice, not the party that requested the review. Likewise, “bill to” identifies the customer receiving the invoice, not the sender of the support message.\n\nPayment reminder generated 10 October: “Dear Emberline Metrics, Quill Software has not received payment. The balance due is $1,080.00 by 15 October.” The reminder is authored by Quill and addressed to Emberline. It is not a request by Emberline that Quill pay money. A line in the adjacent audit export reads “Quill Software requested a refund from payment processor for duplicate bank fee,” but the transaction ID is QP-190 and has no connection to this invoice or this customer. It remains visible only because the export groups all refund-related events together.\n\nReconciliation note: the listed components sum to $720 + $165 + $90 + $25 = $1,000; tax of $80 yields $1,080. The customer challenged one charge, but the review confirmed it and the vendor issued no credit. There is therefore no recorded reduction, payment, or reimbursement in the account as of the invoice date. The customer’s proposed “if the audit confirms an error” clause is conditional, and the audit did not confirm one. Billing helpdesk status: reviewed; invoice unchanged; account payable by Emberline Metrics to Quill Software. No need to send this particular matter to supplier relations, whose staff already have enough invoices to alphabetize.",
