    "state": "Event planning procedures: booking, attendance, and payment\n\nPurpose\nThis page explains the standard planning steps for meetings, receptions, and workshops organized by Bluefin Rentals. The procedures apply to internal staff coordinating an event and to any team member submitting a booking or payment request. The event lead remains responsible for checking the current booking record and communicating confirmed arrangements to participants. A draft plan is not a confirmed booking, and a quote is not evidence that a payment has been made.\n\nVenue bookings\nAn event lead may request a venue by submitting the date, expected attendance range, room layout, access times, and accessibility requirements. The venue team will reply with either a confirmation or a request for more information. A date is reserved only after the venue team sends written confirmation. If a room or date is unavailable, the event lead must choose another option or ask the venue team to suggest alternatives. Mentioning a room in an event proposal does not mean that it has been booked. Changes to the confirmed date, room, or access period must be confirmed in writing before they are treated as approved.\n\nAttendance and invitations\nThe event lead may prepare an invitation list and set an RSVP deadline. Invitations sent are not equivalent to accepted invitations, and accepted invitations are not necessarily equivalent to individual attendance counts. Where an organization can send several representatives, the planning record should use the number of individual attendees supplied through the RSVP form. The event lead should distinguish confirmed responses from pending responses in every update. A forecast can be used for early planning, but it must be labeled as an estimate. The final attendance count should be checked before printing name cards, preparing seating assignments, or submitting a final meal quantity.\n\nCatering and dietary details\nA catering proposal is a quote until the event lead accepts it through the approved process. The proposal should state its quantity, included items, service fees, dietary options, and deadline for changing the order. A listed dietary option is included only when the accepted proposal says it is included. Dietary requests collected from attendees should be sent to the caterer in the form and by the date specified in the accepted proposal. Do not assume that a menu can be changed after the stated deadline. If the caterer requires approval for quantities above the quoted amount, the event lead must obtain that approval before confirming an increase. A preliminary count is not a final order.\n\nPayments and refunds\nThe payment record should separately identify deposits, balances, due dates, and the party receiving each payment. A deposit is recorded as paid only after the payment has been processed and recorded; an invoice or payment request alone does not show payment. Refund requests must be submitted in writing to the organization that received the payment. A question about whether a refund might be available is not itself a submitted refund request. The person coordinating the event should record the request date and the organization’s response. Refund eligibility depends on the written terms for the particular booking; this page does not promise that any payment will be returned.\n\nProgram and equipment\nThe event lead should keep the program, room plan, and equipment list in the current event folder. Items marked proposed remain pending until the responsible provider confirms them. A venue’s standard equipment may be listed separately from equipment the event team must bring. If recording, photography, or livestreaming is needed, the event lead should record whether it has been requested and whether it has been confirmed. These are separate statuses. A request does not establish confirmation, and a general note about available equipment does not establish that a service has been booked.\n\nStatus updates\nA useful update identifies the event date, confirmed arrangements, pending decisions, deadlines, and the owner of each outstanding task. It should not present estimates as final counts or requests as completed actions. If a plan changes, the event lead should update the planning record and notify the relevant service provider. Staff should use the latest confirmed information when answering questions and should not infer approvals from silence.",
