    "state": "Policy page: Temporary schedule adjustments, Granite Dental\n\nPurpose\nThis page explains how an employee asks for a temporary change to their normal start or finish time. It applies to staff in the clinic, billing office, and records unit. A discussion of a possible future conflict is not itself a schedule adjustment. The policy desk records an adjustment only when an employee clearly asks for a change to a particular schedule, and the manager confirms the arrangement under the normal coverage rules.\n\nHow to make a request\nSend the people desk the dates affected, the current hours, the hours requested, and a short explanation. The request should be stated as something the employee wants now. “If my evening class is moved, I might need to start later” is planning information. It does not ask the manager to change any shift. “Please change my start time to 9:30 on the dates listed” is a current request. If the circumstances later change, the employee may send a new instruction.\n\nWhat a manager may do\nA manager may discuss possible options and estimate whether coverage could be available. A manager may not enter a change just because an employee asks what might be possible. The employee must choose whether to request the change. An estimate is not an approval, and a hypothetical discussion should not be recorded as an approved arrangement. This applies even where the manager thinks the future circumstance is likely.\n\nExample from the records unit\nAn employee asks, “If the bus timetable changes next month, could I start at 9:30?” The manager answers that 9:30 might work if coverage is available. The employee then says, “Thanks; I am not asking to change my hours yet. I will contact you if the timetable changes.” The case is information only. The manager should not change the employee’s rota or tell coworkers that the later start has been granted.\n\nThe example is not a promise that all future schedule requests will be accepted. If the employee later submits an actual request, the manager checks staffing, patient coverage, and the dates. A manager should not treat “might,” “if,” “could,” or “would” as a substitute for the employee’s present instruction. Equally, an employee’s current request should not be dismissed merely because it refers to a future date: a request for a definite future schedule can still be current if it is direct and specific. The key distinction is whether the employee has asked for a definite change, not whether the calendar date is later.\n\nRecords and corrections\nThe people desk enters one of three statuses: information only, request pending, or approved adjustment. Use information only for a process question or conditional discussion. Use request pending when the employee directly asks for specified hours but the manager has not decided. Use approved adjustment only after manager confirmation. Do not infer a status from an informal conversation with another employee. If a manager has made a coverage estimate but the employee has not requested a change, the status remains information only.\n\nCommon questions\nCan I ask about a schedule change before I know the dates? Yes, you can ask for information. The desk will not reserve hours based only on that question. If you later know the dates and want the change, send a direct request.\n\nCan I ask my manager to keep an option open? You can discuss possibilities, but the manager should not describe the option as an approved schedule. A possible future need remains possible until you actually ask for a change. The policy does not require an employee to make a request before ready, and it does not allow the desk to invent one on the employee’s behalf.\n\nCan a coworker request for me? A coworker can pass along general information but cannot make the employee’s decision. The case notes should say who made each statement. The employee’s own direct words determine whether a current request has been made. A conditional comment from a coworker is not an employee request, even if it accurately describes what the employee may need.",
