    "state": "FAQ excerpt: Reimbursement and training registration, Quill Dental\n\nThis page is for employees who are considering a course or professional event. It explains the distinction between asking about a possible expense and asking the company to reimburse an expense already incurred. It also covers how to register for a course. A statement about what someone might do if a future condition occurs is not evidence that the action has already happened.\n\nQ: When can I request reimbursement?\nA: Submit the receipt and the completed expense form after you have paid an eligible cost. The finance desk cannot reimburse a cost that has not been paid. Asking “If I attend the workshop, will the company repay the fee?” is a question about a possible future expense. It is not a reimbursement claim, and it does not show that you registered or attended.\n\nQ: Can I ask finance to hold money for me?\nA: No payment is reserved from a question alone. If you want reimbursement after paying, submit the claim with the receipt. A manager may explain the usual limits, but a conversation about a possible payment does not create a claim. The employee should not write “paid” on a form for a fee that is only being considered.\n\nQ: How do I register for a course?\nA: Use the learning portal and select “Register.” A direct request to register now can be reviewed under the training budget. “If the clinic approves the time off, I would like to register” describes an intended later action dependent on approval. Unless the employee actually submits a registration or clearly asks the learning team to register them now, there is no completed registration. A question about what might happen is not a registration.\n\nExample: An employee asks the learning team, “If my supervisor approves the schedule, could I attend the Friday course?” The team replies that it can check availability after the supervisor responds. The employee says, “I am only checking the process today; I have not registered.” The correct record is information only. The team should not tell the course provider that the employee has a place.\n\nQ: What if a colleague says I will probably go?\nA: A colleague’s expectation does not register you or create an expense claim. Only the employee’s direct action or clear current instruction can establish one. Keep a note of who said what. A supervisor’s statement that “she may attend if the schedule works” is a conditional prediction, not an employee’s commitment.\n\nThe ordinary approval sequence is as follows: employee asks about the course or submits a direct registration request; supervisor reviews schedule coverage; learning team confirms a place if available; employee pays any required fee; employee submits a receipt for any eligible reimbursement. Each step is separate. An expected later step must not be recorded as already completed. If the employee asks about a possible reimbursement before paying, answer the policy question and do not open a paid-expense claim. If the employee has paid but is only asking whether a future course might be covered, that separate fact does not turn the hypothetical course into an attended event.\n\nCase note guidance: Avoid shorthand such as “course approved” when the supervisor has only said approval could be possible. Write the actual condition and the current status. “Could attend if schedule approved” is not the same as “registered for Friday.” “Could claim if fee paid” is not the same as “claim submitted.” The desk should not infer either registration or reimbursement from a person’s interest, a course calendar entry, or a colleague’s planning note. Employees may return later with a definite request when the relevant details are known.\n\nA final reminder for supervisors: employees are allowed to explore options without committing. Do not pressure an employee to submit an application merely because they asked what the policy allows. Conversely, if an employee clearly submits a current request, record