    "state": "Policy page: Internal transfer interest and equipment return, Alderwick Services\n\nThis policy page is used by the people team when staff discuss moving between units or changing their assigned equipment. It applies to ordinary internal inquiries, not to a formal disciplinary or medical process. Employees can ask what choices may become available without making a transfer request. The page also says that equipment is returned only when an employee has been instructed to return it or has actually ended the assignment requiring it.\n\nTransfer inquiries\nAn employee may ask whether a vacancy could be suitable if a future event occurs. For example, “If the evening team opens a position, could I move there?” is a question about a possible future option. It is not an application for the vacancy. To apply now, the employee must identify the vacancy and directly ask to be considered. A supervisor cannot submit an application merely because the employee has expressed interest in a possible later move.\n\nThe people team should classify a conversation as information only unless the employee makes a definite current request. “I would consider a transfer if my commute becomes longer” does not establish that the commute has changed or that a transfer is requested. “Please consider me for the posted records role” is a current application, even though the selection decision will come later. An application is not an approval; these are separate statuses.\n\nEquipment return\nThe employee’s assigned tablet remains assigned while the employee continues in the current role, unless the equipment team directly requests its return. A statement such as “If I transfer later, I can return the tablet” is a future commitment conditional on a transfer. It does not mean the tablet has been returned, and it does not itself ask the equipment team to collect it. A current return instruction must state that the employee is returning the device now or ask the equipment team to arrange collection now.\n\nExample case summary\nA staff member tells the people adviser: “If the records opening is still available after the next roster is published, I might apply. If I move, I can hand back my tablet then. For now I only want to know how internal selection works.” The adviser explains the vacancy process. No application form is attached. The staff member’s current role remains active, and the tablet inventory shows the device assigned to that person. The adviser makes no transfer recommendation and sends no equipment collection request.\n\nWhat not to infer\nA manager may say a transfer seems possible if staffing improves. This is not a promise of a vacancy. A colleague may say the employee is “probably moving soon,” but that does not make an application. The employee may talk about returning equipment under a future condition without having returned it. Case notes should preserve the conditional wording rather than compressing it to “transfer” or “device returned.”\n\nRecords should be updated only when the action is definite. For a transfer, the statuses are inquiry, application received, selection in progress, and approved move. For a device, the statuses are assigned, return requested, collected, and inventory closed. Do not skip from inquiry to application received, or from assigned to collected, because a future plan sounds likely. If a later message contains a direct request, use its date and words to update the record then.\n\nSupervisors should answer questions neutrally. They must not tell coworkers that an employee is moving based on a conditional conversation, and they must not remove assigned equipment based on a promise that applies only if a transfer happens. The employee can make an application and an equipment return request separately. One does not automatically imply the other. In particular, a hypothetical transfer does not establish either current transfer intent or completed equipment return.\n\nThe page’s last reminder is that conditional language can include a useful plan but remains conditional. “If the role opens, I might apply” is not the same as “I am applying.” “If I transfer, I can return the tablet” is not the same as “I returned it.” A careful note should say exactly what the employee has done now, and should not convert a possible future event into a completed one.",
