    "state": "Kestrel Health Civic Access Portal: public review and field notes\nReviewer: Wanjiru Haddad\nReview date: 18 September 2025\n\nI used the portal while helping my neighborhood group apply for a temporary street-use permit and while checking whether two members could use the municipal fee-support benefit. I am writing this as a review of the service, not as a request for the city to change a decision. The portal has several useful screens, but the instructions appear to have been assembled from separate departments. Some buttons use the same words for different processes. The page called “My requests” includes permit applications, benefit applications, and general questions, which made it difficult to compare the status of each one.\n\nThe street-use permit page says an applicant should submit a site sketch, a contact number, and the proposed dates. It also says that a permit is not final until the district officer issues a signed notice. A green bar marked “received” only confirms that the upload reached the system. The bar does not mean the permit has been granted. In my case the upload receipt came immediately, while the signed notice arrived later in a separate message. A neighboring association, which had applied for a different block, got a yellow bar asking for a revised sketch. Their case is not mine, though the portal showed both cases in the same dashboard because I was listed as a contact person for the association.\n\nThere is a fee-support benefit for certain permit applicants. The help page gives the ordinary rule as follows: a household with verified annual income at or below 24,000 credits may receive the support, provided the application is filed before the permit is issued. A boxed exception says that a household above that limit may still qualify if it has a current municipal hardship certificate issued before the filing date. A certificate requested but not yet issued does not count. The support is a waiver of the permit processing fee, not a cash payment and not a refund for fees already paid. I found this distinction easy to miss because the dashboard labels the field “financial assistance.”\n\nMy group’s application was filed on 2 September. The permit itself was not issued on that date. The notice came on 12 September, after review of the sketch. Our household income statement showed 25,200 credits. The number was not 24,200, as one earlier draft of the spreadsheet said; I corrected it before upload. We had a hardship certificate, but the copy in the portal had been issued on 4 September. The earlier appointment confirmation was dated 29 August, but it only recorded that we had asked for a certificate. The clerk later explained that an appointment confirmation is not a certificate and cannot be substituted for one.\n\nFor comparison, another applicant in our group had income of 23,600 credits and no hardship certificate. That person filed on 3 September and received a fee waiver. A third applicant had income of 28,000 credits and a certificate issued on 1 September, and also received a waiver under the exception. These examples are included because the help desk used them when explaining the difference between the ordinary threshold and the exception. They are not entries for my household. The portal puts the examples underneath the personal account details, which can make them look like additional household records if a user reads too quickly.\n\nThe fee page also has a narrow refund rule. If a person pays the processing fee before submitting an application, the person can ask for that payment back only when the payment was duplicated by the payment system. A later award of fee support does not create a refund right for a fee already paid. This is stated in a gray note under the payment button. The button itself says “review refund options,” so I initially thought every support award might produce a refund. The help desk confirmed the wording is broad but the written rule is narrower.\n\nMy own payment log showed one successful payment of 180 credits on 1 September and no duplicate charge. I did not ask for a refund. In the community channel, someone wrote, “Great, another fee for the privilege of waiting!” That was sarcasm about the wait and the payment, not praise for the price. A separate message from a volunteer said, “If the permit is denied, I will ask whether the fee can be returned.” That was a future possibility, not an actual refund request. Neither message was written by me, and neither should be treated as part of my application.\n\nThe dashboard’s contact section has caused another confusion. A message from the district office said, “We have not received a signed consent from the applicant.” That means the applicant had not provided consent. It does not mean the city refused to give consent to the applicant. The quoted response below it was from a neighborhood officer who wrote, “I am not refusing to sign; I need to inspect the route first.” This also was not a refusal. The route inspection concerned a separate permit and did not change the financial-support criteria.\n\nI contacted the portal help desk on 10 September. The agent said the application was “complete for review,” then clarified that this phrase did not mean “approved.” The decision screen stayed pending until the district officer signed the notice. When I asked whether the portal could show a separate status for the fee support, the agent said it was planned but not available. My review score would be higher if the city separated the statuses and put the certificate exception beside the income threshold rather than several screens away.\n\nA printed notice in the library says the permit desk closes at 16:30, while the portal’s general contact page says 17:00. The permit desk confirmed the earlier time applies to in-person documents; the later time applies to telephone questions. This is another example of a rule that changes according to the kind of action. It is not an exception to the benefit eligibility rule. Likewise, the public holiday calendar affects processing days but does not change the filing date or the date on which a certificate was issued.\n\nFor applicants trying to plan, the most important points are to distinguish upload receipt from approval, distinguish an appointment from an issued certificate, and read the support rule before paying. The exception is real but has a timing condition: the hardship certificate must already exist before the application is filed. It is not enough to have a certificate eventually, or to have requested one earlier. The case history screen records the date on every uploaded item, which is helpful when a clerk checks whether the exception applies. My criticism is that the screen does not explain why those dates matter until a user opens the separate help article.\n\nAt the end of the review, I asked the civic access office to correct the help page. This is feedback only. It is not an appeal, a new application, a cancellation, a request for a refund, or a statement that the permit is denied. The review system asked whether the portal had made it possible to find the eligibility rule. I selected “partly.” The system then showed a generic confirmation saying the comment had been received. As with an application upload, that confirmation was not a decision by the city.",
