    "state": "Foxglove Dental shipping desk — help page, refreshed for the spring packing run\n\nWe try to make the shipping choices sound less mysterious than they do on some carrier websites. Standard parcel service is the ordinary option for boxes that can travel with the regular delivery van. The current dispatch note says that standard parcels leave the warehouse on Tuesdays and Thursdays. That detail is here because people often mistake a dispatch day for a guaranteed arrival day, and we do not promise a particular arrival date for ordinary parcels.\n\nThe separate cold-chain service is for temperature-sensitive dental materials. On the booking screen, it is called “Chilled courier,” and the carrier keeps the parcel in its monitored cold compartment while it is moving between depots. That is the service name to look for if the shipment needs to stay chilled. A parcel being marked fragile does not, by itself, mean it has been booked for chilled transport. The two labels appear next to each other on the packing sheet, which can be a little confusing when someone is checking a stack of labels quickly.\n\nThere is also a fragile-handling option. It asks the carrier to keep the parcel upright and avoid stacking other cartons on top. It is useful for glass jars, but it does not add temperature monitoring. We mention that because a few suppliers have described their glass vials as “cold stock” in casual emails even when the product can travel at room temperature. The actual booking field, rather than a casual product nickname, tells the warehouse what service was selected.\n\nReturns use a different label from outbound parcels. The returns label has “Return to Foxglove” printed across the top, while the outbound chilled label says “Chilled courier.” If a box is sent back with the return label, that does not mean the original delivery was chilled. Likewise, seeing the word “cold” in a note about the receiving storeroom is not proof that the carrier used the cold-chain option.\n\nFor questions about delivery, please copy the service name exactly as it appears in the booking record. A dispatch receipt may also mention the van route, the packing bench, or the customer’s preferred receiving hours. Those are useful details for locating a parcel, but they are not service names. Our help page is deliberately chatty because the labels are easy to mix up when the warehouse is busy.",
