    "state": "Quill Analytics shipping desk page — parcel collection notes\n\nThis page is for people arranging outgoing collections, and it is written in the casual voice of the dispatch team because otherwise the instructions sound like they were drafted by a stack of boxes. When the booking record says “courier collection,” the driver is meant to collect the parcel from the sender’s named pickup point. The pickup point might be a reception counter, a storeroom, or a side door; use the location written in that booking rather than guessing from the parcel description.\n\nA “depot drop-off” is different. With that service, the sender takes the parcel to the carrier depot. The depot can scan it there, and a scan may appear in the tracking history before the parcel is on a delivery vehicle. A depot scan is not evidence that the courier collected it from the sender. We spell that out because people often see the carrier’s depot name in an update and assume the driver has visited the pickup address.\n\nThe booking summary can also show a delivery preference, such as “leave with reception,” for the parcel’s eventual destination. That wording is about where the recipient may receive the package. It does not tell the sender where to hand over an outgoing parcel. A pickup point and a delivery preference are separate fields, even when both mention reception. If the sender needs to know what to do next, check the service field first, then check the pickup-point field.\n\nFor the ordinary collection option, the confirmation includes a collection window, but a window is not an exact arrival time. The driver may arrive at any point in the displayed window. The page does not promise that a particular named driver will appear, and the driver’s name in an earlier message can relate to another route. Those details can be helpful when the carrier is looking into a missed visit, but they do not change the booked service.\n\nThe examples in this guide include a parcel called “Blue Folder,” a recipient preference marked “reception,” and an old depot scan from a prior shipment. Those are examples of details that can look important in a busy booking screen. For the actual shipment, the service field and pickup point on its own booking record are the useful details. If you are writing in for help, tell us whether the booking says courier collection or depot drop-off, rather than describing only what tracking happens to show.",
