    "state": "Copperleaf Foods card terms, revision 3.2 — excerpt supplied with Yara Sorensen's dispute file\n\nSection 4.1 — A cardholder may ask us to review a posted transaction within 45 calendar days after its posting date. The posting date is day zero; the request date is included when counting elapsed days. A request at 45 days or fewer is timely. Weekends and holidays count as calendar days.\n\nSection 4.2 — A pending authorization is not a posted transaction. An authorization that expires without settlement does not start the review period. If a transaction posts after an earlier authorization, use the posting date, not the authorization date.\n\nSection 4.3 — A customer who recognizes a purchase but disagrees with a merchant's refund decision may request a merchant-resolution review. This is distinct from an unauthorized-use report. The fact that the customer recognizes the purchase does not by itself establish that every line item was accepted.\n\nSection 4.4 — Exception: for a card replaced after suspected theft, the 45-day period is extended only if the posted transaction occurred while the replacement card was in transit. A replacement-card request, by itself, does not qualify.\n\nCase note, prepared October 20: Yara recognizes a $72.00 Copperleaf Foods purchase posted September 1. She says she bought two meal boxes and received both. Her receipt lists $60 for the boxes and $12 for a “priority packing” add-on. She says she did not choose that add-on and asks us to review it after the merchant refused to remove it. She does not say the card was stolen or that the boxes were missing.\n\nThe bank log shows an authorization for $72 on August 31 that expired, followed by the settled $72 transaction posted September 1. A replacement card was requested August 29 because the old card's chip was unreliable; the replacement arrived September 3. No suspected theft was reported, and the purchase used the old card with the cardholder present. The customer submitted