    "state": "FAQ excerpt circulated after an angry service review — Northspan Pump & Boiler\n\nWhy is everyone being told to read this FAQ before sending another complaint? Because the same avoidable dispatch mistakes keep happening, and then customers are blamed for not understanding a rule that our own agents skip. These instructions apply to pump and boiler visits booked through the field desk. They do not replace an individual written service offer where that offer expressly gives the customer a better remedy.\n\nA. When does a return visit count as included?\nA return visit is included when the original technician leaves a listed fault unresolved or fails to perform a required verification. A visit is not “completed” merely because a technician arrived, replaced a part, or collected a signature acknowledging arrival. The exception is a documented access obstruction: if the customer prevents every reasonable attempt to inspect the affected equipment during the booked service period, dispatch may close that visit as customer-blocked. A customer who refuses one unsafe time but offers a reasonable alternative during the same service day has not prevented every reasonable attempt. Do not write “refused access” unless the customer actually refused all offered access.\n\nB. What if a customer asks for money back?\nAn immediate refund is a request to return a payment now. A conditional credit is different: the customer may say that a credit will be sought only if a later event occurs. Do not record that future condition as a current refund request. Likewise, a customer who says “I do not want a refund; I want the repair” has not requested a refund just because the word appears in the sentence. Exception: if the customer explicitly asks for both a repair and an immediate refund, record both requests; wanting a repair does not cancel a separately stated refund demand.\n\nC. Which tests must be recorded?\nRecord each verification required by the work order. If the work order lists a pressure test and a leak check, both are required unless a specific safety hazard makes one test unsafe. In that case, the technician must identify the hazard in the report and receive supervisor approval before omitting the test. A missing signature does not itself mean the customer refused the test, and a signed arrival slip does not prove acceptance of the repair. Do not infer an approved exception from a technician’s vague note that the customer was “unavailable.”\n\nD. What is not a service defect?\nA stain, dent, or noise mentioned in a message is not automatically a defect in the equipment being serviced. Check which item the customer identified. A photograph of a dent on a nearby storage cabinet does not establish damage to a boiler. A customer can report a real pressure fluctuation even if it occurs only occasionally; the word “intermittent” does not mean “not reported.” Conversely, saying “there is no leak” is not a leak report. If a customer says a sound is not coming from the pump, do not classify it as a pump-noise complaint unless another statement identifies the pump as its source.\n\nE. What does the seven-day callback promise mean?\nThe clock starts on the calendar date the initial visit occurred, not the date an agent later opened a case. The promised callback must occur within seven calendar days, counting the day after the visit as day one. A later appointment offer is not a callback already completed. If the seventh day is a Sunday, the promise remains seven calendar days unless the written offer expressly moves the deadline. A specific offer that says “within seven business days” controls for that customer; weekends are then excluded, but public holidays are excluded only if the offer says so.\n\nPlease stop turning exceptions into shortcuts. A customer’s anger does not prove a defect, but it also does not erase a clearly stated fault. Do not close a work order on the basis of a proposed future action, a quoted agent note, or an exception whose conditions have not been met. When the evidence is mixed, record what the customer actually said and what the technician actually did. The FAQ is not permission to turn “not yet,” “unless,” or “if” into “already.”",
