    "state": "Service agreement SA-88 for the Ashwick cold-room controls, prepared by Tidewater Rentals and signed by Ines Mensah, facilities lead. The customer is furious about repeated paperwork, so this copy includes the amendments actually agreed; superseded language is retained below for audit clarity.\n\nBase clause 4.1, dated 2 February: Tidewater will inspect the cooling controller and replace a failed sensor during the scheduled visit. The visit is complete only when the controller is tested under operating load and the sensor reading is recorded. If the customer prevents access to the plant room for the entire appointment window, Tidewater may classify the visit as blocked. A refusal of one proposed entry time is not a prevention of access if the customer offers another time within the appointment window.\n\nBase clause 4.2: The customer may request a fee refund if Tidewater fails to provide the contracted service. A request made only on the condition that a future deadline is missed is not an immediate refund demand. This distinction must not be removed from dispatch notes. If the customer expressly requests an immediate refund as well as continued service, both demands must be recorded; one does not silently cancel the other.\n\nAmendment 1, 18 February, initialed by both parties: The controller load test may be omitted only if performing it presents a specific documented electrical hazard. The technician must describe the hazard and obtain written approval from the service supervisor before treating the visit as complete. The customer’s signature on an arrival tablet does not waive the test. A technician’s statement that the customer was “difficult” or “busy” is not hazard documentation.\n\nAmendment 2, 3 March: The original promise of a return visit “within five days” is replaced with “within five calendar days after the first visit.” The date of the first visit is day zero; the next calendar date is day one. No weekend extension applies. This amendment does not change the separate deadline in an individual work order.\n\nWork order WO-191, issued 9 March: First visit scheduled for 11 March, 10:00–12:00. Required work: check the controller under load; replace the sensor if it is outside the stated tolerance; inspect the condensate alarm. The visit note says the technician arrived at 11:37, replaced the sensor, and did not test the controller under load. It also says “site denied entry.” The attached access log records that Ines declined entry during a scheduled chemical wash at 11:20 and offered entry at 11:45, still within the appointment window. The technician left at 11:42, before the offered time. There is no hazard report or supervisor approval. The service portal’s green status flag is automatically set when a part is installed; it is not a completion certification.\n\nCustomer email, 12 March: “I did not sign off on the repair. I said the sensor alarm stopped, not that the controller passed its load test. I am not asking for an immediate refund. If your return technician does not come by 16 March, I will ask for the service fee back. I want the test first. The black mark in my attached picture is on the pipe insulation, not a crack in the controller housing. Please stop copying ‘customer denied access’ into every reply.”\n\nWork order amendment, 13 March: Tidewater offers a return visit on 16 March, 09:00–10:00. A visit completed on that date is inside the five-calendar-day promise: after the 11 March initial visit, 12 March is day one, 13 day two, 14 day three, 15 day four, and 16 day five. If no return visit occurs by the end of 16 March, the customer may then request the fee refund. The deadline does not convert the conditional email into a refund request before it expires.\n\nInternal review, 14 March: The supervisor rejected the “site denied entry” classification. The offered 11:45 access was within the original window, and the technician had not documented an unsafe test. The installed sensor does no