    "state": "Excerpt from the Vellum Harbor Mutual property-claims service terms\n\nSection 8. Reporting and item identification\nA policyholder must identify each item for which reimbursement is sought and provide a description of its condition after the reported event. A general statement that “the room was affected” does not establish that every item in that room was damaged. When a schedule lists several items, the evidence for one scheduled item must not be treated as evidence for another merely because both were present at the same location.\n\nSection 9. Damaged property and incidental records\nFor purposes of claim intake, “physically damaged property” means property for which the submitted account describes a physical alteration or impairment attributable to the incident. A request for payment, a replacement estimate, or an item’s inclusion on an inventory is not, by itself, a report of physical damage. Documents, labels, and receipts may be separately identified as damaged contents when their own condition is described. A damaged receipt does not establish damage to the goods named on that receipt.\n\nSection 10. Personal property and borrowed goods\nA policyholder may submit a claim for property the policyholder owns or is otherwise entitled to insure under the policy. Borrowed property is not automatically the policyholder’s property merely because it was stored at the insured premises. This excerpt does not determine whether a particular policy includes an endorsement for borrowed goods; the submitted claim account must identify the relevant interest before that question can be answered.\n\nSection 11. Scope of this excerpt\nThese provisions govern classification of the submitted account, not payment approval, valuation, or the cause of an event. If a claim account describes an item as intact, a separate damaged item elsewhere in the account does not override that description. A reviewer should use the particular item’s own condition statement. For this exercise, no additional policy schedule or endorsement is supplied.\n\nClaim account attached to this excerpt: Policyholder Leena Orlov reports that a burst pipe wet a storage shelf. Her signed item statement says, “The brass desk lamp was on the shelf but remains clean, dry, and fully functional. I request no payment for the lamp.” She separately reports that a stack of printed labels on that shelf became soggy and unreadable, and she seeks reimbursement for those labels. The same account mentions a borrowed camera case that was stored in the adjoining room; it reports that the case is dry and undamaged. An intake cover sheet, not signed by the policyholder, calls the whole storage area “water-damaged contents.” The item-specific account and the excerpt’s identification rules are the evidence for classifying these entries.",
