ts may use their own brushes. The supply page lists communal brushes but says nothing about personal ones. | Instructor to clarify. | Open\n\nDiscussion copied beneath the rows: “We should not mark every question about cost as a request for a refund. Ticket A-20 is a request to explain a line, and A-15 is a question about what is included. If someone asks for a refund, quote their words in the notes so we have a clear record.” Another staff member wrote: “A calendar question is also not a schedule-change request. A-16 asks if Saturday is running; it does not say the learner wants to transfer.”\n\nA note about status labels says that “open” means the office still owes an answer, not that the learner has submitted every possible request connected to the course. “In progress” means someone is preparing a response, not that the learner’s underlying issue is fixed. The ticket summaries were shortened for the workbook, so details not present in a row should be confirmed with the original sender rather than invented.\n\nThe coordinator’s wrap-up: “I know the phrase ‘field-kit charge’ makes people think about payment, but there are several different questions someone might ask about a charge. A person can ask what it covers, whether it is included in the price, whether it is optional, or whether it should be returned. Those are not interchangeable. Keep the actual request attached to the ticket.”\n\nLoose note: “For A-22, do not tell the learner personal brushes are allowed until the instructor replies. The communal brush listing is the only current information. For A-19, the positive comment is nice but it doesn’t mean the learner has confirmed where notes are posted. I think that’s all. The sheet’s rows are sorted by course, not by priority, and there isn’t a separate flag for billing requests yet.”",
