ut is printed automatically, and it does not say that a learner has already requested a copy unless a message is actually received.\n\nQuestion: Who can update my contact details?\nAnswer: The course office can help with profile changes after verifying the learner’s identity. If an email address has changed, provide the current address through the secure portal or follow the verification instructions. A message saying that an address is old is not enough for staff to change it without checking.\n\nA paragraph from the office’s internal guide says: “Keep an individual’s request separate from the options described in the FAQ. We often explain several possible routes, but those explanations are general. Do not write in a learner’s file that they chose a route unless they did. A person might ask about paper copies without requesting one, ask about group availability without requesting a transfer, or ask what a charge covers without seeking a refund.”\n\nEditor’s comment: “This can feel fussy, but it saves everyone trouble. We had a case where someone copied the whole answer about absences into a learner’s record as if the learner had asked for a recording. They had only asked what to do if they could not attend. We should not promote possible options into reported facts.”\n\nOne more note: “When the FAQ itself is the only evidence provided, it tells us what a learner could do, not what a particular learner has done. If a message or record does not mention a request, the answer should remain unrecorded. Please don’t infer a request from the fact that the office has a process ready for it.”",
