nd water seemed warm | Leila found the cooler unplugged at the wall. She plugged it in, waited while it started, and explained that the water needed time to cool. The unit was still running normally when she finished. | Receptionist said she understood and would check the temperature later. | No part required.\n\nTab: Notes from Leila\nI got through all six calls without needing to borrow anything from the warehouse, which was nice because the storeroom person was at lunch when I first arrived. The fan was the only job that involved opening a guard, and I put every screw back before testing it. The scanner cradle issue was just its feet being uneven; the scanner was not broken and there was no request to swap it. The bench lamp gave us no repeatable fault while I was there, so I haven’t marked it repaired; it is logged as checked and left for monitoring. The label printer’s head was dusty, and the test after cleaning looked good. The wall clock adjustment was a setting change, not a replacement. The cooler had power disconnected, and it started once plugged in. I didn’t promise that the water would be cold immediately. I wrote each customer outcome down as the person at that site described it, and the signatures are attached to the workbook. The billing column is blank unless a charge or a billing conversation actually happened. None of these rows contains a request for a refund, credit, or payment reversal. Please use the row number if you need to ask me about one; otherwise the sheet is ready for closeout.",
