    "state": "FAQ excerpt: Sorting out appointment changes without making a meal of it\n\nPeople ask whether moving a visit means they have cancelled it, and, honestly, the calendar makes that look more dramatic than it is. If you ask the desk to move your appointment to another available time, the original slot is released but the appointment remains active under the new time. A cancellation is different: you have to say you do not want the visit at all, and the desk records it as cancelled. Please don’t assume that a message saying “I can’t do Tuesday” is enough to cancel; staff will ask whether you want to reschedule.\n\nFor this FAQ, a request counts as a cancellation only when the patient explicitly says they no longer want the appointment, not when they decline one proposed time or ask for a different one. If a patient says, “I do not want to cancel; I just need Thursday instead,” record a reschedule. We know the wording can feel a bit circular, but the distinction matters when reminders go out.\n\nA separate question is whether the visit is confirmed. A visit is confirmed when the patient or authorized contact replies affirmatively to the offered time. If the person says they have not confirmed, staff must leave it pending. An unanswered reminder is not a confirmation, and a note that says “no response yet” is not a sneaky yes. The system may still display the reserved time while pending, which is why people sometimes think it has been settled.\n\nFinally, an appointment is marked as a telehealth visit only if the booking record explicitly says video or telephone. A patient asking whether video is available does not make the visit telehealth. That’s the whole thing; easy to mix up when reading a busy schedule, but staff should follow what the record actually says rather than guessing from a question or a tentative message.",
