    "state": "Incident postmortem: The reminder that looked like a cancellation\n\nYesterday’s scheduling wobble was caused by a note that got read too quickly, which is an unglamorous way to spend a morning. At 8:10, the front desk received a portal message from Callum Iyer about his Thursday follow-up. The message said, “I’m not cancelling the appointment. I can’t make the morning slot, so please see if there is anything after lunch.” The first staff member released the Thursday morning slot while looking for alternatives, which was permitted, but the appointment itself should have stayed active.\n\nAt 8:24, the scheduling screen showed the visit as cancelled because the operator selected the wrong status from a crowded dropdown. The screen’s color change made that status look final, and the next reminder batch omitted the visit. Nobody had actually told the patient that the appointment was cancelled, and the patient had not asked for it to be cancelled. The mistake was found when another staff member searched the open follow-up list and noticed that the message contained the phrase “not cancelling.”\n\nThe desk called Callum and offered an afternoon time. He replied, “Yes, keep the appointment, just not in the morning.” The corrected record now shows the follow-up on Thursday afternoon. The old morning slot is no longer held, but that does not mean the follow-up was abandoned. We have added a short reminder beside the status menu: releasing a time and cancelling a visit are different actions. It sounds obvious written down, but the dropdown manages to make obvious things weird.\n\nFor the review, we checked whether the visit was ever clinically completed. It was not; the patient had not attended yet. We also checked whether he asked to withdraw from follow-up. He did not. The issue was an administrative status error, not a change in his intention. The callback and corrected booking resolved the immediate problem, though we still need staff to read the whole sentence instead of stopping at “can’t make.”",
