    "state": "FAQ excerpt: A few less obvious bits about reminders\n\nThe reminder service is useful, but it isn’t a mind reader, so please don’t treat a message sent by the service as proof that a patient agreed to anything. A reminder can be generated while an appointment is still pending. The booking becomes confirmed only after the patient replies yes, or an authorized contact accepts on the patient’s behalf. A reply saying “I have not agreed to this time” leaves the booking unconfirmed even if the reminder screen turns green for some unrelated reason.\n\nRescheduling has its own little wrinkle. If someone says, “I don’t want to cancel, but I need a different day,” staff should keep the visit active while searching for a new slot. If a new time is not available right away, the record may say “reschedule requested,” not “cancelled.” A patient who says they no longer want care is different; staff should ask once to make sure the request is intentional, then record the cancellation if confirmed. Don’t infer that from “Tuesday won’t work.”\n\nWe also get questions about reminder preferences. A patient can ask not to receive text reminders without asking to stop all reminders. The preference is specifically about text messages unless the patient says they do not want reminders by any channel. If they say “Please don’t text me; email is fine,” email reminders remain allowed. This is one of those details that seems obvious until someone reads only the first half of a note.\n\nFor chart status, “not completed” means the visit has not yet taken place, or the clinician has not marked it done; it is not a synonym for cancelled. Likewise, “not cancelled” does not mean confirmed. These are separate fields, and a record can be active but still awaiting the patient’s answer. Staff should preserve those distinctions even when the schedule is crowded and someone is asking for a quick answer at the desk.",
