    "state": "Tidewater Labs\nPeople Operations — Quarterly staffing meeting notes\nDate: 14 May\nChair: Wanjiru Mensah\nRecorder: People Operations\n\nThe meeting reviewed the process for temporary schedule adjustments, the status of several requests, and the wording to be used in the revised manager guide. Attendees were reminded that a request is not an approval and that informal coverage arrangements do not amend an employee’s recorded schedule. The group agreed to keep the existing distinction between a manager’s acknowledgement and a formal authorization by People Operations.\n\nPolicy excerpt read aloud: “A temporary schedule adjustment is authorized only when People Operations records an approval in the case register. A manager may propose an adjustment but cannot authorize it. An entry marked ‘not declined’ is not, by itself, an approval; the register must say approved.” Wanjiru asked that the phrase “not declined” be retained because staff had repeatedly treated it as equivalent to approval. The group confirmed that the phrase was intended to describe an unresolved or non-rejected request, not to grant permission.\n\nCase review, item 17: A laboratory coordinator asked to shift the start of her Thursday shift from 8:00 to 9:00 for four weeks. Her supervisor replied, “I have no objection to discussing this, and I am not opposed to a temporary trial if People Operations signs off.” The coordinator then wrote, “Thanks, I understand that you are not refusing the proposal.” The supervisor entered “not declined” in the local team tracker. The People Operations register, checked during the meeting, showed the case as “awaiting review”; there was no approval entry. The schedule therefore remained unchanged. No one disputed that the request had been made, but the group explicitly separated the existence of a request from its authorization.\n\nCase review, item 18: A different employee had asked whether a shift change might be possible later in the summer. The manager’s note said, “No change requested for the current roster; do not treat this inquiry as a submitted request.” The register contained no case. The meeting agreed not to infer an active request from a hypothetical question about a future possibility. The guide should say that a request must identify a proposed change and a period, but this item was not treated as a current application.\n\nThe team also reviewed status labels in the shared tracker. “Not closed” means that the case has not been closed; it does not mean that the request was accepted, rejected, or fully assessed. “No objection recorded” means only that no objection appears in the manager’s comments. Neither label substitutes for the register’s decision field. Wanjiru noted that the combination of a positive-sounding phrase and a missing decision had led to confusion in two earlier audits. The revised guide will state the status labels in full rather than relying on shorthand.\n\nAdministrative follow-up: People Operations will publish a one-page explanation of the register fields. Managers will be asked to avoid writing “approved unless someone objects,” because silence is not approval under the quoted rule. The register administrator will retain the date and author of every formal decision. The group declined to make retroactive entries based on recollections of hallway conversations; if a decision was not recorded, the administrator must refer it for review rather than infer that it occurred.\n\nThe meeting closed with a reminder that an employee may still submit a request while the status is pending. A pending request is not an authorized schedule change, and a manager’s lack of refusal does not change that outcome. No general policy change was adopted at this meeting.",
