vices received the notice.”\n\nInternal comment, 10:44, Nadia Okafor: “For clarity, the employee has not said that the return date is uncertain. The message confirms it. The phrase ‘not provisional’ in the case system means the date has been confirmed; it does not mean the employee is not returning. The employee is returning on the recorded date. Please keep the ticket limited to whether our receipt requirement is met.”\n\nA copied chat excerpt from a scheduling channel reads: “If payroll cannot finish validation by Friday, we may need to ask the employee whether another date works.” A later line from the channel owner states: “That was a contingency only. No alternative date has been proposed or requested, and we have not told the employee to change the confirmed date.” The ticket owner asked that this excerpt remain attached because it explains why a payroll follow-up was opened, but it does not alter the record’s confirmed status.\n\nResolution note, 11:20: The case desk verified the receipt timestamp and the written confirmation. Payroll’s acknowledgement remains pending. The team will follow up with payroll through the regular queue, without reopening the employee’s return-date decision. The support ticket is not a request for a leave extension, payroll correction, or new schedule arrangement. No exception to the stated return-notice rule was invoked. The desk closed the review of the receipt question while leaving the separate payroll task open.\n\nAudit reminder: Staff must not collapse “received,” “validated,” and “acknowledged” into one status. The record can be received while a later administrative action remains unfinished. Likewise, a blank acknowledgement field is not evidence that a notice was never submitted. Where a date is genuinely provisional, the system must say so; the notes here report the opposite.",
