    "state": "Bluefin Analytics\nInternal support ticket — leave administration\nTicket BA-7716\nOpened: 18 September\nAssigned group: People Operations\nSubmitter: Xiu Rahman\n\nRequest summary: Please check whether the case record supports a reimbursement for dependent-care expenses during approved leave. The employee is not asking for a new leave approval, and the request is not for ordinary commuting costs. The ticket was opened after the employee noticed two different descriptions of the reimbursement rule in the staff portal and the manager handbook.\n\nApplicable policy excerpt from the current handbook: “Eligible expenses are reimbursed only when the employee submits itemized receipts for care provided during an approved leave period and the expense is not otherwise reimbursed. The monthly cap is 240 credits. Expenses for ordinary commuting are excluded. A receipt is not inadequate merely because the provider’s invoice uses a shortened trading name, provided the date, service, and amount can be matched to the approved provider record.” The handbook further states that a portal summary cannot override this rule.\n\nInternal comment, 09:05, Leave desk: The leave period was approved in the case system. The employee submitted receipts dated within that period for dependent care from a provider listed in the system. The receipts show the service and amounts. The provider uses a shortened trading name on invoices, but the account reference matches the approved provider record. The desk has no evidence that another program reimbursed these expenses.\n\nInternal comment, 09:30, Payroll review: The submitted eligible amounts are 86 credits, 79 credits, and 93 credits. The total is 258 credits. The cap is 240 credits per month, so the payable amount for this month cannot exceed 240 credits. A separate commuting claim of 18 credits appears in the employee’s draft worksheet, but that line is not included among the dependent-care receipts and is excluded by policy. Do not combine that commuting amount with eligible care expenses or use it to alter the cap calculation.\n\nInternal comment, 10:10, Xiu Rahman: “The portal card says ‘care reimbursement, up to 300 credits,’ but the handbook in force for this case gives a 240-credit cap. The employee has not received another reimbursement. The phrase ‘not otherwise reimbursed’ is satisfied on the evidence currently available; it does not mean the employee must wait for a denial from every unrelated benefit program.” The team confirmed that this interpretation is consistent with the full policy text attached to the ticket.\n\nA manager’s note from an earlier thread reads, “If Finance later identifies a duplicate payment, we may need to recover the overage.” This is a conditional warning, not evidence that a duplicate payment has occurred. No recovery has been opened, no duplicate is recorded, and the employee has not been told that reimbursement is denied. The ticket is for the current submission only.\n\nThe case desk compared dates, service descriptions, and amounts against the uploaded receipts. All three care receipts fall within the approved leave period. The receipt names can be matched to the approved provider record using the account reference. The commuting line remains outside the eligible category. The desk has not approved payment above the cap and has not calculated any separate entitlement for another month. The actual reimbursement decision remains subject to the normal finance posting step; this ticket asks what amount is supported under the stated monthly rule.\n\nClosure note: Eligibility evidence is sufficient for the care expenses, and no other reimbursement is shown. Finance should process no more than the applicable monthly cap. The portal’s higher summary figure is not controlling. The employee’s draft total must not be treated as the payable total, since it includes an excluded line and exceeds the cap. The evidence does not support a finding of duplicate reimburse