    "state": "Tidewater Labs\nPeople Operations — meeting record and action register\nMeeting date: 22 November\nSubject: Access badges during extended absences\nChair: Tomasz Sorensen\nRecorder: Workplace Services\n\nThe meeting was convened to clarify when a staff access badge must be suspended during a leave of absence. The attendees reviewed the current access policy and recent questions from team administrators. The policy excerpt circulated in advance states: “A badge is suspended when an employee begins an approved absence lasting more than ten consecutive calendar days, unless the absence is designated as an approved remote-work period. The threshold is more than ten days, not ten days or more. A manager’s reassurance that access is unnecessary does not itself create a remote-work designation.” The policy administrator confirmed that the quoted text is the current version.\n\nThe group first reviewed the calendar-day count method. The first day of the approved absence counts as day one. Weekends and holidays are included. A separate medical appointment or a non-working day does not pause the count unless the case system records a return to active work. The group rejected a proposal to count only scheduled workdays because that would contradict the written rule. The ten-day threshold and the exception must both be checked before the access status is changed.\n\nCase Alpha: The case register shows an approved absence beginning 2 October and an approved return to active work on 12 October. The record therefore covers 2–11 October as absence days: 2 October is day one and 11 October is day ten. A team note says, “The badge need not be suspended, since the employee was not away for more than ten days.” The register has no remote-work designation. The access administrator confirmed that the note is consistent with the threshold: an absence of exactly ten consecutive days does not exceed ten days. No badge suspension was entered for this case.\n\nCase Beta: Another register entry shows an approved absence beginning 3 October, with return to active work recorded for 14 October. The absence days are 3–13 October inclusive: 3 October is day one and 13 October is day eleven. The employee worked remotely for two days during the period, but the case system does not designate the period as approved remote work. The manager wrote, “We did not need to suspend access because the employee could connect from home.” The group noted that the policy exception depends on an approved remote-work designation, not on informal ability to connect. The badge remained active by mistake and was referred for correction.\n\nCase Gamma: A third employee had an absence period longer than ten days, but the register explicitly marks the entire period as an approved remote-work period. The badge was not suspended. The chair emphasized that this is the stated exception, rather than evidence that every extended absence can remain active. The entry contains both the approval and the remote-work designation; no inference from a manager’s comment is needed.\n\nDuring discussion, someone proposed treating the phrase “not ineligible for continued access” as meaning that access was automatically permitted. The policy administrator clarified that this wording only indicates the absence of a stated disqualification; the actual threshold and exception still control. Likewise, a note saying “no suspension request received” does not establish that suspension is unnecessary. Access decisions are generated from the recorded absence dates and designation fields, not from whether a supervisor objects.\n\nAction register: Workplace Services will correct the Beta badge record after confirming the dates and notify the manager. The administrator will leave Alpha unchanged and retain the day-count calculation in the audit entry. Gamma will remain active under the approved remote-work exception. No general exception was adopted. The meeting also directed staff to stop using “not away long enough” without listing the dates, because that shorthand has produced inconsistent interpretations. The minutes do not alter the policy or authorize retroactive remote-work designations.",
