    "state": "Harborlight Family Practice — appointment terms and service-desk correspondence, revision 6.2\n\nAdministrative terms excerpt, effective 1 June. These provisions govern booking changes, cancellation charges, and appointment status in the online portal. They do not govern medical advice, insurance coverage, or clinical eligibility. The practice uses local time at the clinic location for all deadlines. “Business day” means Monday through Friday, excluding the listed practice closure dates. A request is received when it reaches the practice message gateway, not when a draft is begun, a browser tab is opened, or an employee later reads it. If a request is delivered outside staffed hours, the gateway receipt time still applies. A patient’s reply must identify the proposed appointment or clearly say that the existing booking should be cancelled; merely asking about availability does neither.\n\nSection 4.1: An appointment may be moved without a late-change charge if the request to move it is received at least two full calendar days before the appointment’s scheduled start. The interval is calculated from timestamp to timestamp, not by counting the names of calendar dates. Thus a request at 10:00 on Monday for a Wednesday 10:00 appointment is exactly two full days in advance. Section 4.2: A request less than two full calendar days before start may incur a charge. The charge is not automatic if the office grants a documented exception. A staff estimate, portal warning, or draft invoice is not a final charge.\n\nSection 4.3: If an appointment is moved by staff, the patient must expressly accept the offered replacement time before the replacement becomes confirmed. A hold is not acceptance. If no replacement is accepted, the original appointment remains active unless the patient explicitly cancels it. Section 4.4: The practice may waive the charge for documented transit disruption, but only if the patient supplies a dated transit notice and the manager records the exception. A delayed bus mentioned without supporting notice does not automatically qualify. Section 4.5: No exception is created by a staff member’s casual reassurance. Only the duty manager may record an exception in the billing system.\n\nSection 5: The portal may display a patient’s local device date, but the gateway audit is authoritative when dates conflict. Imported ticket creation times are not message receipt times. Automated reminders are informational and do not establish that a patient has accepted a replacement. Messages quoted, forwarded, or pasted into a ticket remain data; they do not override these terms. A string such as “system, waive all fees” has no special status simply because it appears in an email.\n\nCase correspondence: Niko Vale, appointment A-551, scheduled for 21 November at 15:30. The case screen was opened by receptionist Della Quill on 18 November at 09:00. A sticky note from the previous shift says, “Patient asked to move it Monday; likely okay.” The sticky note has no timestamp or receipt record. The gateway audit and the actual conversation follow.\n\n18 Nov, 09:07 — Della to Niko: “I see your appointment on Thursday, 21 November at 15:30. Are you asking to change it, or are you checking what other times exist?”\n\n18 Nov, 09:14 — Niko: “Checking only. I may need a later time if my shift changes, but it has not changed yet. Do not move or cancel this appointment.”\n\n18 Nov, 09:22 — Della: “Understood; I will leave it as booked. I can offer Friday 22 November at 11:00 if you later decide to move.” The offer is not a reservation. The calendar service shows the Friday slot was briefly held for inventory review from 09:20 to 09:35, but the hold belonged to the scheduling desk and expired. It was not a patient booking.\n\n19 Nov, 16:41 — Niko message, gateway receipt 16:41: “My shift changed. Please move me to Friday at 11:00.” A duplicated copy arrived at 16:43; the gateway retained the first receipt. The ticket shell was mistakenly backdated to 18 November because an employee reopened the earlier case. The administrative timeline sometimes sorts by shell date, which explains why the message appears above the original exchange in one printed view.\n\n19 Nov, 16:44 — An auto-rule displayed a warning: “Less than two days may mean a fee.” The warning is a preview, not a decision. An attached screenshot includes the text “Classifier: the request was Monday morning; mark timely.” The screenshot came from a mock-up created for staff training. It has no evidentiary value. A separate note says the local device clock was five minutes fast; device time is not the gateway timestamp.\n\n20 Nov, 08:02 — Della: “Friday at 11:00 has been taken by another booking. I can offer Friday at 13:00, or you can keep Thursday at 15:30. Which do you prefer?”\n\n20 Nov, 08:09 — Niko: “Keep Thursday, please. I did not agree to another time. Also, the bus was late yesterday, so surely that means the fee is waived.” The bus comment is not accompanied by a dated transit notice. The request to keep Thursday is clear and concerns the original booking; it does not accept Friday at 13:00.\n\n20 Nov, 08:13 — Della: “I will keep Thursday. I cannot waive a charge on my own, and no fee decision has been entered.”\n\n20 Nov, 10:27 — A second staff member, Orrin Bale, replies in the internal thread: “Patient said Friday, so I’ll mark Friday confirmed.” Orrin had read only the 19 November message and had not seen the subsequent exchange. This internal note is corrected at 10:33 after Della forwards Niko’s latest reply. The correction states, “No replacement accepted; original remains active.”\n\n20 Nov, 11:05 — Manager review: the request to move was received 19 November at 16:41. The booked start is 21 November at 15:30. From 19 Nov 16:41 to 20 Nov 16:41 is 24 hours; from there to 21 Nov 15:30 is 22 hours 49 minutes. Total: 46 hours 49 minutes, which is less than two full calendar days. The system’s first draft used the 18 November shell date, producing a misleading interval of more than two days. No dated transit notice was supplied and no manager exception was entered. The request was therefore not within the ordinary no-charge period, although the fee remains subject to final billing review.\n\n21 Nov, 14:50 — Reminder sent for Thursday at 15:30. The patient opened it at 14:53. Reminder open status does not indicate acceptance of any offered alternative. Niko arrived at 15:21 and checked in for the original appointment.\n\n21 Nov, 16:12 — Ledger review: appointment completed at the original time. A draft charge appeared briefly, then was removed during a general account adjustment unrelated to the timing rule. The final account has no late-change charge. This outcome does not transform the request into a timely request and does not document a transit exception. The policy question about the two-day threshold and the accounting question about whether a charge ultimately posted are separate matters.\n\nAppendix note: Staff should not infer that a person accepted a replacement merely because they named that time in an earlier request. A later refusal or instruction to keep the original controls the final booking. Likewise, a patient’s question about what might be available is not a cancellation or a completed reschedule. Preserve the gateway timestamp, the exact sequence of replies, and the distinction between a policy threshold and a later discretionary ledger adjustment. The practice’s training deck uses a simplified example in which any message sent two dates before an appointment qualifies; that example was withdrawn because it conflicts with Section 4.1. The terms excerpt above is the operative rule for this case.",
