e does not know who made it. She is not saying she caused it, but she is also not saying nobody could have caused it; she simply cannot identify how it happened. The move-in photographs show a similar faint mark. She did not ask us to repaint the wall, and she does not want a damage claim opened over it. We can leave the existing photo with the inspection record.\n\nFinally, she asked what counts as an urgent repair. I gave the example of a leak that cannot be stopped, and she said the lobby door is not that kind of situation. She is not saying it isn’t worth checking; she is saying it can wait for the routine visit. That distinction sounded clear. She also joked that the door has “a stronger opinion about closing than she does,” which was just her way of describing the sticky closer, not a claim that the door is entirely broken.\n\nMy follow-up is to add the lobby closer to the ordinary maintenance list and send the larger-print statement next month. There is no request to revise the current ledger, no key handover, and no new claim about the wall. Priya was relaxed throughout and repeatedly said she wanted the record to be accurate rather than dramatic. If the summary is shared with another staff member, it should preserve her wording: the door does close, but she wants its closer checked; the charges are accepted; and the mark is documented without a damage allegation.",
