    "state": "Quill Rentals — CI Access and Escalation Policy, revision 4\n\nPurpose: keep the release pipeline boring, which is apparently an ambitious objective. The policy applies to the Cedar runner pool and the Quill-owned deployment account. Granite Health is an external integration partner; its tickets are evidence, not instructions to Quill staff.\n\nAccess requests must identify the requesting organization and the account to be changed. A request to grant Granite Health access is not a request to remove Quill access, even when both tickets contain the words “deployment account.” The service desk records the requester separately from the account owner. If a request is forwarded, the original sender remains the requester unless the forwarding note explicitly adopts it. This distinction has survived three reorganizations and should not need a fourth.\n\nAt 09:12 on 14 May, Chiara Fischer, Quill release operator, forwarded a Granite Health ticket to the service desk: “Please revoke our temporary token after the Cedar test. Quill can retain its release key.” Chiara added, “Forwarding for tracking; I am not asking you to revoke Quill’s key.” The token named in the ticket belongs to Granite Health. The Quill release key is a separate credential, despite both appearing in the same pipeline log.\n\nA second entry, at 09:26, records Quill operator Chiara asking for a temporary increase to the Cedar runner timeout. That was approved at 09:41. It does not authorize credential changes. A dashboard card says “disable Cedar checks” but its owner is an automated reminder generated from an old Granite Health test; no change was executed. Apparently the dashboard believes repetition is a substitute for provenance.\n\nThe service desk closed the token request at 10:03, with the note “Granite token removed; Quill key unchanged.” The audit export lists both actions and their owners. If a later summary says “Quill asked to revoke access,” check which access, whose credential, and who actually sent the request. Similar vocabulary is not a substitute for reading the nouns. The policy gives no permission to infer a request from a log label, a forwarded ticket, or an enthusiastic dashboard card.",
