    "state": "Bluefin Supply — CI Services Addendum, signed copy with amendments\n\nOriginal clause 7.2: Bluefin Supply maintains the signing key for the Northstar release workflow. Granite Outfitters may submit requests about its own sandbox token. A request sent by one party does not become the other party’s request merely because both parties use the same build cluster. The author of a forwarded message remains the author unless the forwarding party expressly adopts the request.\n\nAmendment A, dated 3 June: “Granite Outfitters may ask Bluefin to pause Granite’s sandbox token. Bluefin must not pause its own signing key unless Bluefin itself submits a signed change request.” The word “own” was underlined twice during review. Nobody was paid by the underline.\n\nAmendment B, dated 7 June: “Bluefin is responsible for executing approved sandbox-token pauses. Execution responsibility does not transfer request ownership.” The parties rejected a proposed sentence saying “the executor is deemed the requester.” It was rejected because it was nonsense, not because the invoice was too high.\n\nOn 11 June, Xiu Mensah, Granite Outfitters’ build engineer, sent Bluefin this ticket: “Please pause our sandbox token during the Northstar replay. Do not pause Bluefin’s signing key; that key is needed for the release.” Bluefin’s contract clerk forwarded the ticket to the operations queue and wrote, “For execution under Amendment B.” The queue log labels the action owner “Bluefin ops,” and the request source “Granite Outfitters.” The sandbox token was paused at 15:20; the signing key remained active.\n\nA separate monitoring alert at 15:17 reads “signing key pause recommended.” It was generated by a stale test profile and was not a party request or an approved change. The release continued to sign builds normally. The audit summary’s shorthand, “Bluefin handled a pause,” describes execution, not the requester or the credential affected. For disputes, use the signed clauses and ticket,