    "state": "Meeting notes — Granite Health / Quill Rentals pipeline review, 22 July\nAttendees: Greta Tanaka (Granite Health), Kwame Sorensen (Quill Rentals), build and release staff. The recorder captured enough detail to make a short meeting feel like a long meeting, a useful technical achievement.\n\nGreta opened with the Granite Health staging failure: the Cedar job had retried four times after a test fixture returned a malformed response. She asked Quill to disable Granite’s staging webhook until Granite replaced the fixture. She did not ask Quill to disable Quill’s production webhook. Those endpoints share a dashboard heading, because naming things distinctly remains optional in this particular interface.\n\nKwame said Quill would own the change ticket and perform the disable operation, subject to the normal approval. He clarified that executing a partner’s request did not make it a Quill request. The minutes record the requester as Granite Health and the executor as Quill Rentals. The webhook was disabled at 14:10; the production endpoint stayed live. A nearby alert, “production webhook failing,” was traced to a separate synthetic monitor that had been misconfigured and did not prompt any change.\n\nOn the cost of reruns, Greta reported that Granite’s staging jobs had used 18 runner minutes each over three failed attempts, plus one successful 11-minute run. She asked Quill to add a cost estimate to the follow-up, not to reimburse Granite or waive any invoice. Kwame said Quill would calculate the estimate but would not accept a payment obligation in the meeting. The recorder’s draft line “Quill takes costs” was corrected to “Quill calculates costs.” One verb apparently saved everyone a meeting with counsel.\n\nThe group also agreed that Granite would replace the fixture and submit a fresh request before its webhook was re-enabled. “If the fixture fails again, we will cancel the integration,” Greta said; this was recorded as a contingency, not a cancellation.