    "state": "Granite Outfitters — Cedar CI incident record, 9 September\nIncident owner: Leila Alvarez, Granite Outfitters. External reporter: Bluefin Supply. Runner pool: Cedar-2. The incident screen puts “owner,” “reporter,” and “person who pressed the button” in adjacent columns, which is efficient if one enjoys confusion at speed.\n\nAt 08:40, Bluefin Supply reported that its integration test had failed after a certificate expired. The report asked Granite Outfitters to rotate Bluefin’s sandbox certificate. It explicitly said that Granite’s release certificate should remain untouched. Granite’s runbook confirms that each organization owns its own certificate; Granite can perform the rotation for Bluefin with approval, but does not thereby become the requester.\n\nAt 08:52, Leila opened the change ticket, quoting Bluefin’s report and marking Bluefin as request origin. At 09:05, Granite’s security reviewer approved rotation of the Bluefin sandbox certificate. Leila performed it at 09:19. The Granite release certificate was not rotated. A separate scheduled scan at 09:14 flagged the Granite certificate as “near expiry”; the certificate actually had 26 days remaining, and the scan generated no approved change request. The scan’s warning is included because the dashboard insists that every warning deserves a paragraph.\n\nThe test then passed. Bluefin’s reporter confirmed the integration was working and asked Granite to close the ticket. Leila closed it at 09:37. The audit trail distinguishes three roles: Bluefin requested the sandbox rotation, Granite approved and performed it, and the scanner merely emitted an alert. A copied summary says “Granite rotated its certificate for Bluefin.” That sentence is ambiguous at best and wrong if “its” means Granite’s own certificate. The key record identifies the affected credential as Bluefin sandbox, owner Bluefin, action performed by Granite.\n\nA later chat message from Bluefin says, “Please don’t rotate anything else until