    "state": "Bluefin Supply — release-room notes, 3 October\nPresent: Chiara Bergstrom (Bluefin Supply), Leila Alvarez’s replacement on the Granite Outfitters integration team, and the Cedar build crew. The chair asked everyone to distinguish requests from actions, since last month’s notes managed to make a button press sound like a contractual amendment.\n\nBluefin’s nightly integration failed because the Granite Outfitters staging endpoint returned a stale schema. Chiara, for Bluefin, asked Granite to roll back Granite’s staging adapter to build 6.8. She did not ask for a rollback of Bluefin’s production adapter. Granite engineer Niko accepted the request and initiated the rollback at 21:16. The audit record names Granite as actor and Bluefin as requester. The two adapters have nearly identical labels; this is evidently considered a feature.\n\nDuring the same call, Granite’s dashboard displayed a red card reading “Bluefin production rollback recommended.” The card came from a rehearsal job running against a mock service. It was not a request from Bluefin and no production action followed. A second card, “Granite staging rollback complete,” accurately reflected the action. The team agreed to suppress the rehearsal card, although nobody expects the interface to become modest.\n\nChiara also asked whether Granite could provide a rollback duration for the incident report. Granite’s first estimate was 9 minutes for stopping the job and 17 minutes for deployment; a separate unrelated Bluefin cache restart took 12 minutes. The final audit measured the requested Granite rollback as 26 minutes total. The arithmetic is not complicated, but the cache restart is nearby and therefore apparently irresistible. Granite agreed to report the duration, not to reimburse Bluefin for the failed run.\n\nChiara said, “If the same schema error returns tomorrow, we may ask for a production freeze.” The group recorded this as a possible future request, not a present freeze request. No pro