    "state": "Foxglove Health — incident policy addendum, kindly circulated by Dmitri Okafor\n\nDear colleagues, and thank you very much for keeping this small operational matter pleasant. The standard service-credit rule is as follows: for each complete hour of qualifying downtime, the customer receives 2% of the monthly subscription fee, capped at 10%. A partial hour earns no credit. The clock starts when the monitor confirms the outage and ends when the service is restored; a customer’s estimate is not the clock. Credits are calculated from the $500 monthly fee shown on the account ledger, not from the annual invoice total. I have included this so nobody has to guess, which would be terribly relaxing but not especially useful.\n\nAt 09:07, the monitor recorded the API as unavailable. It recovered at 12:22. The customer later wrote that it had been down “since breakfast,” which was a wonderfully precise unit of time, and suggested that we round the event up to four full hours. The official interval is three hours and fifteen minutes, so only three complete hours qualify. At 2% per hour, that is 6% of $500, or $30. The 10% cap would be $50, so it does not change this result. A worksheet copied into the ticket shows $50 beside “possible credit”; that is the cap, not the calculated credit.\n\nThe customer also asked for a credit for a separate 18-minute delay in loading the dashboard. The service rule says separate intervals cannot be combined unless the monitor shows one continuous outage. It does not: the dashboard delay was logged the following afternoon, and the API remained available. Thus the 18 minutes do not add to the three-hour event. The customer’s note says, “What a delight to lose half a morning and then get a generous thirty dollars.” We should understand that as dissatisfaction, not sincere delight.\n\nFor clarity, the outage was not caused by the customer’s own scheduled maintenance, which would be excluded under section 4.2. The log shows no such maintenance. The draft email’s statement that “we owe $50 because the outage was basically four hours” is an informal estimate and conflicts with the complete-hour rule. Please retain the monitor timestamps, the ledger fee, and this addendum with the case. Thank you ever so much for resisting the temptation to make the arithmetic more cheerful than it is.",
