    "state": "Typed-up handwritten note from the Quill Software incident desk, with thanks to Wanjiru Rahman\n\nPlease forgive the uneven layout; I copied the paper log as carefully as I could. We had a truly wonderful Tuesday, if one considers repeated login failures a delightful team-building exercise. The subscription’s monthly fee is $800. Our incident policy promises 1.5% of that fee for each complete 30-minute block of verified customer-facing outage, up to a maximum of 6%. Blocks are counted separately within each incident; do not round minutes up. The page headed “customer report” may include guesses, but only the monitor record establishes the duration.\n\nMonitor note, incident Q-41: service degraded from 10:11 to 11:41, then recovered. That is exactly 90 minutes, or three complete 30-minute blocks. Calculation: 3 × 1.5% = 4.5%; 4.5% of $800 = $36. The maximum possible credit is 6% × $800 = $48. Someone pencilled “$48—round up generously,” which is not the policy calculation. I cannot recommend generosity as a unit of time, however kind it sounds.\n\nCustomer’s message, copied verbatim: “Marvelous, only three blocks of being locked out. I could not have asked for a more convenient morning.” The account owner later clarified that this was a complaint. The customer did not say the service was actually convenient. They requested a service credit, but did not request a cash refund. Please do not put the word “refund” in the request-type box merely because a credit and a refund both involve money.\n\nA second monitor line concerns incident Q-42: a five-minute delay in one report export. It occurred two days later, while login worked normally. The report-export delay is explicitly excluded from the login-outage calculation and cannot be attached to Q-41. If counted on its own, five minutes is zero complete 30-minute blocks and produces no credit. The scratch note “90 + 5 = 95; still three blocks” reaches the same block count by accident, but combining distinct i