    "state": "Quill Software — service review memo, very kindly assembled by Ines Tanaka\n\nThank you for your patience with this review of the Acorn tenant’s two API incidents. The plan offers a credit of $5 for each complete set of 250 failed requests in a single incident, provided that incident has at least 500 failed requests. Credits are capped at $25 per incident. A successful request that needed a retry is not a failed request. Please use the final monitor counts, not the preliminary estimates pasted into the ticket. It would be absolutely splendid if the numbers could be made to agree by wishing, but that is unfortunately not an accounting method.\n\nIncident Lark, Monday: the first monitor estimate was 1,100 affected requests. Final log: 640 failed after all retries, 300 succeeded on retry, and 160 were never submitted because the client paused its own queue. The final failed count is 640. The eligibility threshold is met. There are two complete sets of 250 in 640, with 140 left over, so the credit is 2 × $5 = $10. The cap is $25 and does not change that result. A draft says “1,100 / 250 = four credits”; it uses the preliminary affected total and rounds up. Neither step follows the policy.\n\nIncident Wren, Wednesday: final monitor log shows 490 failed requests. The account note calls it “practically five hundred,” which is a charming description but not an exception. The incident is below the 500-request minimum and earns no credit. Do not pool it with Lark: they have different incident IDs and occurred on different days. A combined count of 1,130 would still not be a valid per-incident calculation. The report also lists 80 successful retries from Wren; these are not additional failures.\n\nThe customer wrote, “Wonderful work: I especially enjoyed having requests vanish twice.” The project lead confirmed this was sarcastic criticism, not praise. In the same email, the customer asked for a service credit for Lark and said nothing about cash repayment. The reimbursement checkbox was selected in an early draft, then crossed out; the final request field says “credit.” Please do not treat the crossed-out selection as the customer’s request. The unusually polite sign-off—“With deepest appreciation for this elegant outage”—does not alter the stated dissatisfaction. Thank you for keeping preliminary totals, retry successes, and the final request type distinct.",
