    "state": "INCIDENT POSTMORTEM — Juniper Quay, building C, 14 May\nPrepared by: M. Senn, site coordinator. Typos left in.\n\nAt 07:42 a resident in C-214 reported water across the kitchen threshold. The first call was logged as “sink overflow,” but the resident later clarified that the sink bowl was dry and the water was coming from the ceiling seam beside the cabinet. Maintenance arrived at 08:03. They shut the kitchen cold-water riser serving C-314 and C-214; the drip stopped within six minutes. The upstairs occupant, Talia Wren, said she had not used the kitchen tap that morning. She had run the dishwasher at 06:50, though.\n\nThe contractor opened the cabinet panel upstairs at 09:15. A loose dishwasher inlet coupling had sprayed against the wall, then tracked down the pipe chase. The coupling was not cracked. It had been hand-tightened during a filter replacement on 2 May, and the work order had no torque check or photo. The dishwasher pan contained water. The upstairs floor was damp under the unit, but no water had reached the hallway or bedroom. C-214 had wet cabinet trim and a warped kickboard; no personal belongings were damaged. The resident moved food away from the cabinet and stayed in the unit.\n\nThe building has a written response rule: call the on-call technician within 15 minutes for active water entering a finished living area; an incident is “contained” only after the source is isolated and a second check finds no new water for 20 minutes. The first call came at 07:42; dispatch was 08:03. At 08:11, the technician wrote “stopped.” The follow-up check began at 08:18 and ended at 08:38, with no fresh drip. So the response was late by six minutes, but containment met the stated test. The property supervisor initially marked the response compliant because the riser was shut promptly after arrival. That reading was corrected in review.\n\nThere was a separate concern about the resident’s lease. A note on the work order says, “Charge tenant for all water; lease says tenant responsible.” The lease addendum actually says residents may be billed for damage caused by their careless act, but not for ordinary wear, hidden plumbing failure, or a vendor’s incomplete work. The site manager has not assigned fault and has not posted a charge. The dishwasher coupling had been serviced by a vendor, and the record does not establish who left it loose. Do not treat the note as a final billing decision.\n\nDuring the first hour, the front desk told the downstairs resident that the upstairs occupant had flooded the apartment. That statement was unsupported. The supervisor called back at 10:06, corrected the account, and said the cause was still being reviewed. The upstairs occupant was asked to avoid the dishwasher until the contractor returned. She agreed. The downstairs resident asked for a hotel; the manager explained that the unit remained usable and offered a dehumidifier instead. The resident accepted it but asked that cabinet repair be scheduled before the next rent cycle.\n\nAt 12:30, the contractor replaced the coupling washer, ran a rinse cycle, and observed the pan and chase. No leak appeared. At 16:00, the building engineer checked C-214 with a moisture meter; the cabinet base remained elevated compared with the adjacent dry panel. A second reading was planned for the following afternoon. The insurer was notified as a precaution, not because coverage or a claim amount had been determined. No other apartment reported water. The postmortem action list assigns a photo-and-tightness checklist to the maintenance lead, due 21 May; a script correction to the front desk, due 17 May; and a cabinet repair appointment to the site office, date pending. The incident is closed for emergency response, not for repair or responsibility review.",
