    "state": "INCIDENT POSTMORTEM — Flint Orchard Apartments, roof and access review\nCompiled 22 September by facilities clerk. Short forms and spelling kept from the notes.\n\nIncident window was 19 September, beginning with a call from apartment 8E at 17:26. Resident reported a brown ceiling mark after a hard rain, and said a small drop had landed on the window sill. They did not report water on the floor, damage to furniture, or a power problem. The intake agent chose “roof leak—active” from a dropdown. That category triggered the emergency rota even though the caller said the drip had stopped before the call. Rota technician arrived at 18:02 and found the sill dry. A second resident in 8D called at 18:14 about a damp patch at the opposite end of the corridor; the patch was cool and dry when checked.\n\nThe roof inspection record from 17 September says the north drain was partly blocked by leaf litter. A contractor cleared it at 11:40 that day and poured two buckets of water through the outlet; both drained. The work note says “clear today, recheck after rain.” It does not say the roof membrane was replaced. A separate roof repair in June covered the west stairwell, not the north stack. The post-storm inspection found the north drain open, no pooled roof water, and no active entry. Moisture readings in 8E were 21 at the ceiling stain and 11 on adjacent paint; the instrument guide says readings above 18 merit repeat checks, not automatic demolition. The clerk scheduled another reading for 23 September.\n\nA building access complaint came in on 20 September, not during the rain response. Resident in 2A said the side-gate latch sometimes failed to catch. The gate was not locked shut; it swung closed and could be pulled open without a key. A technician adjusted the strike plate at 10:10 and tested it ten times. It caught on nine tries; on the tenth, the gate bounced back. The technician marked the adjustment “partial” and ordered a replacement closer. Delivery estimate is 26 September. The main entrance and garage door were not reported faulty. A handwritten note on the ticket reads “all doors broken”; the reporter later said they meant the one side gate.\n\nThe lease appendix has a maintenance reporting clause: “A resident must promptly report visible water entry. Reporting does not itself establish resident fault, nor does an initial category selection decide whether a charge applies.” There is no claim that anyone in 8E caused the stain. The roof and drain are common-area components. Another note says that a resident’s failure to report a known, ongoing leak may affect responsibility for additional damage, but the report says the drip had already stopped and no added damage is recorded. The supervisor rejected a proposed ceiling-repair charge pending the next moisture reading and contractor review.\n\nThe call log also contains a nearby but unrelated item from 8C: a bathroom fan rattled on 18 September, and a fan replacement was completed 21 September. It had no ceiling moisture and no link to the storm. At 08:35 on 21 September, resident of 4F sent a sarcastic portal line, “Brilliant, another serene night listening to that alarm.” The alarm was a smoke alarm chirping because its battery was low; staff replaced the battery at 09:02. The message was not a compliment, and it did not concern roof water or door security.\n\nReview found that the rota response to 8E was appropriate for the intake category but the category should be corrected to “post-event inspection.” The emergency callout charge is waived when no active leak is found and the resident promptly reports a recent event; the billing rule was applied and no callout charge was entered. The manager will check the damp reading on 23 September, ask the roofing contractor to examine the north flashing, and confirm the gate closer delivery. No resident has been charged for the stain, and the side gate remains usable with an unreliable latch until the replacement part arrives.",
