    "state": "Westmere Evening School course office report: registration and class administration\nReporting period: 1–30 November 2025\nPrepared by: course office team\n\nPurpose\nThis report summarizes routine administration for the school’s short adult courses. It is intended for the next course-office worker, who may need to answer basic questions about registrations, class dates, rooms, and the status of student records. The report does not make decisions about grades or learning outcomes. It records what the office received and what staff entered into the course system. All names and course figures in this training record are fictional.\n\nCourse: Practical Letter Writing\nInstructor: Dalia Meren\nRoom: Study Hall 2\nScheduled meetings: five\nFirst meeting: 4 November\nLast scheduled meeting: 2 December\nRegistration status at report close: open for late registration through 3 November\nThe office received 18 completed registration forms by 31 October. One further form arrived on 1 November and was entered the same day. The roster therefore showed 19 registered participants before the first meeting. A note from the instructor mentions that 20 chairs were arranged in the room, but chair count is not the same as the registration count. The room was prepared with spare seating, and the spare chair does not represent an additional registration.\n\nCourse: Introductory Study Planning\nInstructor: Kemi Voss\nRoom: Seminar Room 1\nScheduled meetings: three\nFirst meeting: 6 November\nRegistration status: closed\nThe roster contains 12 registered participants. Two people asked by email whether late registration was possible, but neither completed a registration form. The course office replied that registration was closed. The inquiries were stored in the general correspondence folder and were not entered on the participant roster. A separate notice for the December study-planning course advertises 15 places; that notice is not the roster for this November course.\n\nCourse: Everyday Spreadsheet Skills\nInstructor: Orin Vale\nRoom: Computer Lab C\nScheduled meetings: four\nFirst meeting: 11 November\nRegistration status: open until 10 November\nThe system shows 14 participants registered as of 10 November. One person withdrew before the first meeting and the roster was updated to 13 active participants. A different person asked to move to a future class but did not complete the school’s withdrawal form. The course office left that person on the roster pending confirmation. At report close, 13 participants were marked active and one was marked withdrawn. The initial count of 14 should not be confused with the active count after the recorded withdrawal.\n\nCourse: Community History Notes\nInstructor: Mara Ellin\nRoom: Reading Room\nScheduled meetings: two\nFirst meeting: 18 November\nRegistration status: cancelled by the school before opening registration\nNo participant registrations were accepted for this course. The course page had been created as a draft and briefly appeared on the internal calendar, but it was removed before students could submit forms. The room and instructor fields were retained in the archived draft for administrative reference. Their presence in that draft does not mean a class was held or that anyone registered.\n\nCourse office procedures used this month\nA registration is counted when the school receives a completed form and staff enter it in the roster. A question about whether a place is available is not a registration. A roster count is distinct from the number of chairs prepared, the maximum room capacity, and the number of people who visit the course page. If a withdrawal is processed, the active roster count is reduced and the record remains available in the history. The report may show both an earlier count and a current count, and staff should use the date and status labels provided.\n\nSchedule and room checks\nThe office compared the published timetable to each approved course record. Practical Letter Writing uses Study Hall 2. Introductory Study Planning uses Seminar Room 1. Everyday Spreadsheet Skills uses Computer Lab C. Community History Notes remains an archived draft and was not delivered. A facilities note says that Computer Lab C has 16 workstations. This is the maximum equipment count, not a participant count. Another note says Study Hall 2 has 24 chairs. That figure is also room capacity, not enrollment.\n\nStudent record handling\nCompleted registration forms are kept in the restricted course-office cabinet. The monthly summary includes totals but does not attach the forms. Email inquiries are kept separately unless a completed form is later received. Withdrawal forms are linked to the participant’s roster entry. Staff should not infer attendance from registration alone. This report does not provide attendance totals. It also does not identify which participants attended each meeting. The instructor may maintain a separate attendance sheet, but no attendance figures are included here.\n\nEnd-of-month checklist\n- Practical Letter Writing roster reviewed: 19 registered participants.\n- Introductory Study Planning roster reviewed: 12 registered participants.\n- Everyday Spreadsheet Skills roster reviewed: 13 active and one withdrawn.\n- Community History Notes checked: no registrations; course was not delivered.\n- Room capacities checked separately from rosters.\n- Late inquiries not counted unless forms were completed.\n- Draft calendar entries not treated as delivered courses.\n\nA finance clerk sent a brief note that the school received 19 course-fee payments for Practical Letter Writing by the report date. That payment count agrees with the 19-person roster at that time, but fees are managed separately from room preparation. The report does not say that the fees for the other courses were all paid. The office is not asked to reconcile payments in this document. Any payment question should be referred to the finance record, not answered by treating a course capacity as an amount collected.\n\nStaff handover note\nWhen answering general questions, use the course name and report-close status. For Practical Letter Writing, the relevant roster number is 19. For Introductory Study Planning, it is 12. For Everyday Spreadsheet Skills, use 13 active participants and note one withdrawal if the question asks about the earlier count. For Community History Notes, the course was not delivered and no registrations were accepted. Do not use the number of seats, workstations, inquiries, or course-page views as a substitute for a registration count. This report is complete for the stated reporting period and contains no pending roster changes at close.",
