    "state": "Course administration operations log and appendix for Larkfield Skills School\nMonth covered: January 2026\nCompiled for: school office handover\n\nThis document combines routine entries from the course calendar, room desk, and registration desk. The entries are included in date order where possible. It is not an invoice, contract, or student evaluation. The office uses the log to keep a clear record of which classes were scheduled and which basic administrative tasks were completed. A course can appear in a planning list before it is confirmed, so the status field should be read with the entry rather than inferred from the title alone.\n\nCalendar extract\nCourse title: Beginner French Conversation\nStatus: confirmed\nRoom: Meeting Room A\nMeetings: Mondays, 5:30 p.m.\nFirst date: 12 January\nThe instructor booking and room booking were both confirmed on 5 December. The registration desk recorded 16 completed enrollment forms by 9 January. A message asking whether the course had a waitlist was received from one prospective student, but no additional enrollment form came with that message. The course coordinator replied that the class had 16 registered participants and that the office would contact the person if a place became available. The person was not added to the roster during the period covered by this log.\n\nCalendar extract\nCourse title: Digital Notes for Study\nStatus: confirmed\nRoom: Computer Room 2\nMeetings: Thursdays, 4:00 p.m.\nFirst date: 15 January\nThe room has 18 computers. The registration roster contains 17 enrolled participants. One computer is kept available for the instructor and technical support. A facilities worker wrote “18 seats available” in a room note, referring to the computer-room equipment. The roster count remains 17. The course began as scheduled, and the calendar entry was not altered during the reporting month.\n\nCalendar extract\nCourse title: Planning a Small Research Project\nStatus: confirmed\nRoom: Meeting Room C\nMeetings: Tuesdays, 6:00 p.m.\nFirst date: 20 January\nThe roster contained 11 participants when registration closed. On 16 January, one participant withdrew using the school form. The course system now shows 10 active participants and retains the withdrawn entry in its history. The instructor’s printed list, made on 12 January, still shows the earlier count of 11. For current administration questions, the system’s active count at month end is 10. The earlier printout was not corrected because it was already distributed, but it is not the current roster.\n\nCalendar extract\nCourse title: Local Archive Introduction\nStatus: draft, then removed\nRoom: Reading Room 1\nProposed first date: 27 January\nThe course was considered for the winter programme but was not approved for delivery. The calendar clerk removed the draft on 8 January before public registration began. There are no completed enrollment forms, no confirmed instructor, and no room booking. A planning spreadsheet still contains the proposed title and date because that spreadsheet is kept as a record of ideas considered. Its presence does not make the course a confirmed class.\n\nCalendar extract\nCourse title: Basic Map Reading\nStatus: confirmed\nRoom: Meeting Room D\nMeetings: Fridays, 2:00 p.m.\nFirst date: 23 January\nThe roster contains 9 completed registrations. The office received two calls asking about the course, but callers did not submit forms. The attendance sheet records 8 people at the first meeting. Registration and attendance are separate counts: one registered person was absent at that meeting. The class was still confirmed and held, and the active roster remained 9 at month end.\n\nDesk procedures\nThe registration desk enters a person on a course roster only after receiving a completed enrollment form. A phone inquiry, an email question, or a web-page view is not counted as an enrollment. The facilities desk records the capacity and equipment of rooms. Those numbers describe what the room can accommodate, not how many students enrolled. The instructor may record attendance at each meeting, which is another separate count. A participant who withdraws is no longer part of the active count, although the historic entry remains in the system.\n\nThe calendar desk uses four status labels. “Confirmed” means the school approved the course and the room and instructor arrangements were recorded. “Draft” means a course is being considered but is not yet approved. “Removed” means the planning item was taken out of the active calendar. “Completed” is used only after the scheduled teaching period ends. The January classes listed as confirmed were active scheduled courses during this report. The Local Archive Introduction item was a draft that was removed; it was never a delivered course.\n\nRoom and roster appendix\nMeeting Room A capacity: 20 chairs. Beginner French Conversation roster: 16.\nComputer Room 2 equipment: 18 computers. Digital Notes for Study roster: 17.\nMeeting Room C capacity: 14 chairs. Planning a Small Research Project roster at close: 10 active, one withdrawn historically.\nReading Room 1 capacity: 12 chairs. Local Archive Introduction: no booking and no roster.\nMeeting Room D capacity: 16 chairs. Basic Map Reading roster: 9; first meeting attendance: 8.\nThe figures in this appendix are repeated from their source records to help staff locate them. Room capacities do not revise course rosters. Attendance at one meeting does not revise the enrollment count unless a separate withdrawal form is received and processed.\n\nHandover checks completed 31 January\n- Beginner French Conversation: confirmed, 16 registered.\n- Digital Notes for Study: confirmed, 17 registered.\n- Planning a Small Research Project: confirmed, 10 active, one withdrawal retained in history.\n- Local Archive Introduction: removed draft, no registration.\n- Basic Map Reading: confirmed, 9 registered; first meeting attendance was 8.\n- Prospective-student inquiries were reviewed and were not counted as registrations without completed forms.\n\nAdditional office notes\nA calendar export contains an old label “winter workshop” beside the Local Archive Introduction title. That label was used during early planning and does not indicate that a workshop took place. A room diagram labels Computer Room 2 as an 18-computer room, not an 18-student course roster. A staff member’s handwritten note says “French class maybe 17?” but the note was written before the completed-form check and is not supported by the roster. The verified roster is 16. The office should use the checked roster figures listed in the handover checks.\n\nThe report contains no tuition payment totals and does not establish whether individual students attended every meeting. It does not include grades, instructor evaluations, or course completion certificates. If staff need to answer a question about the count enrolled, they should use the roster figure. If they need to answer a question about the number physically present on a specific date, they should use the attendance record for that date. The distinction is included because the school’s monthly files often display both types of number next to the course title.\n\nRecord ownership note\nThe course office maintains the roster and calendar. The facilities desk maintains room capacity information. Instructors maintain attendance sheets. Each record has a separate purpose, and a value from one record should not be copied into another without an explicit update. For the January handover, the verified roster figures and course statuses above are the current administrative record. No later correction was received before this log was closed.",
