    "state": "Subject: Kestrel Outfitters service-credit review and deployment chronology\nFrom: Beatriz Iyer, Operations\nTo: Service Reliability and Account Support\nDate: 18 June 2025\n\nPlease use the chronology below for the quarterly service-credit review. The governing service schedule grants a credit only when a qualifying production interruption lasts more than 90 consecutive minutes. The interval starts when the public status page first confirms customer impact and ends when that page records restoration. Internal investigation before confirmation and post-restoration monitoring do not count. A separate notification target asks the duty lead to notify customers within 30 minutes of confirmed impact; missing that target does not change the credit calculation. All times below are UTC.\n\nAt 08:41, the deployment channel recorded that release 6.8.2 had completed. The release added a dashboard filter and changed no billing configuration. A support ticket at 08:49 reported a slow chart, but the service was not yet classified as interrupted. At 08:56, the on-call engineer began checking database connection counts. The status page entry at 09:04 was the first confirmation of a production interruption affecting report exports and dashboard refreshes. The page said that exports might time out and that the team was investigating. The customer notification was sent at 09:28, within the separate notification target.\n\nThe investigation log recorded a queue backlog at 09:17, a cache restart at 09:46, and a temporary improvement at 10:02. The improvement was not considered restoration: the status page remained in an investigating state, and export requests still failed intermittently. At 10:21, a second engineer reported that the backlog had returned. A support representative asked whether a small number of users could retry; that advice was not a restoration notice. The status page was updated at 10:37 to say that the team had isolated a worker-pool issue. At 10:49, the worker pool was expanded. Monitoring showed improving success rates, but the service remained listed as interrupted while the team checked whether queued exports were processing.\n\nThe status page recorded restoration at 10:35 in an initial draft prepared for review. That timestamp was copied from the first improvement note and was not the public restoration time. The published status history, retained in the audit export, shows the relevant sequence: confirmed impact 09:04; investigating updates 09:37, 10:12, and 10:37; restored 10:44. The 10:49 worker-pool expansion was a later stabilization action, not the end of the confirmed interruption. The audit export is authoritative when a draft and published history differ.\n\nQuoted thread:\n\n> From: Support, 11:02\n> The customer asks whether the service credit applies. I see a draft saying 10:35, but the published page may have a different time. Please confirm which record controls.\n\n> From: Reliability, 11:16\n> Use the published history, not the draft or the last mitigation action. The 09:04 entry is the first confirmed-impact time. The 10:44 entry is the restoration time. The schedule uses elapsed consecutive minutes, not the number of updates or the time the first workaround was tried.\n\nFor context, a separate scheduled maintenance window began the previous night at 23:00 and ended at 23:20. It was announced in advance and had no overlap with the incident. The chart complaint at 08:49 was not itself a confirmation, and the team does not use ticket creation time to start the contractual clock. The customer’s account was otherwise eligible for service credits throughout the month. No exclusion or exception in the schedule applies to this incident. Please record the result using only the published incident interval and the stated 90-minute threshold.",
