    "state": "Voicemail transcript, recorded 7 August 2025, 16:10 UTC\nMailbox: Northwind Foods service operations\nCaller: Dalia Fischer, platform operations lead\n\n“Hello, this is Dalia. I am leaving this message to settle the maintenance-window record before tomorrow’s customer review. The subscription addendum allows maintenance without an availability incident only when the customer notice is sent at least 48 hours before the scheduled start. The scheduled start in the approved change record is 09:00 UTC on 6 August. The period is scheduled to end at 09:30. Please use the actual sending time of the customer notice, not the time somebody drafted it, the time the change request was approved, or the time the customer opened it. Our operations log is in UTC, including the email gateway entries.\n\n“The draft message was written on 3 August at 08:30 and saved in the ticket. It was not sent then. The change request was approved on 3 August at 09:05. The outbound gateway shows that the notice was actually sent on 4 August at 09:20. The customer opened the notice at 09:22, and the delivery receipt came back at 09:21. Those latter events are useful for confirming delivery, but neither changes the sending timestamp. I heard someone say the draft date should count because the customer eventually received it well before the window. That is not what the addendum says.\n\n“There is another maintenance record in the folder, from July. It began at 09:00 on 10 July and its notice was sent at 08:45 on 8 July. That earlier record passed the notice test, but it is a different event. Do not carry its status over to the August window. Likewise, the proposed window in an early August ticket was 11:00; it was replaced in the approved change record by 09:00. The actual scheduled start for this test is the approved 09:00 time, not the superseded proposal.\n\n“The release engineer started a dry run at 08:40 on 6 August. That was internal preparation, not the scheduled customer maintenance start. Customer-facing work began at 09:00 and finished at 09:27. There was no extension, and the service returned to normal by the scheduled end. I am not asking whether the work itself was successful; this review is only about whether notice was sent far enough in advance under the stated rule.\n\n“The interval to measure begins at the timestamp the gateway records for sending and ends at the approved scheduled start. Count elapsed hours, not calendar-date labels. The rule says at least 48 hours, so exactly 48 would qualify. It does not say two business days, and there is no weekend adjustment. No exception is listed for a notice drafted earlier, approved earlier, delivered quickly, or opened by a customer. Please write the result as a simple pass or fail and use the actual records rather than recollection.\n\n“One more caution: the customer success note says, ‘We told them on Sunday, so we should be fine.’ That note is referring to when the draft was prepared, not when the gateway sent it. The mail trace and approved change record are the controlling records. The test is straightforward once those timestamps are paired. Please send your determination to the review folder when complete.”",
