    "state": "Incident postmortem: Saltmarsh Outfitters authentication degradation\nDocument owner: Sven Kaur, incident coordinator\nReview date: 22 September 2025\n\nSummary and measurement rule\nOn 18 September, some tenants experienced repeated authentication failures. The customer-facing response commitment in the incident policy is four elapsed hours from the first confirmed customer impact to the first mitigation that restores successful login for affected tenants. A mitigation counts only when the status log records that the affected login path has recovered. A diagnostic action, deployment start, or report that a fix is ready does not stop the clock. This is the response commitment being audited here; overall incident resolution is reported separately.\n\nChronology, all times UTC\nAt 13:02, an automated monitor noticed a rise in token-refresh latency. The monitor did not yet identify failed customer logins. At 13:11, support received a ticket saying that one user had been asked to sign in again. At 13:18, the incident commander opened an investigation. The first confirmed customer impact was entered on the status log at 13:26, after the team reproduced failed logins for two tenant accounts. That is the start time specified by the policy.\n\nAt 14:03, a configuration rollback began. The change reduced latency for newly issued tokens but did not restore login for users with affected sessions. At 14:29, an engineer marked the rollback task complete; the status log still said that customers could not reliably authenticate. At 15:04, the team began rotating a signing key. The key rotation finished at 15:31, but a verification test against the affected tenant path failed at 15:39. These events were included in the incident timeline because they explain the response, but none meets the policy’s recovery condition.\n\nAt 16:12, the status log recorded successful login tests for the affected tenants after a cache invalidation. The incident commander confirmed that this was the first mitigation restoring successful login. At 16:28, support reported that several customers had completed sign-in without assistance. The incident remained under observation. At 17:06, the team marked the incident resolved after checking delayed token refreshes and reviewing the queue. Resolution is not the stop time for the response commitment, because the policy expressly stops at the first qualifying mitigation.\n\nEvidence checks\nA copied spreadsheet lists 15:31 as “recovery,” based on completion of key rotation. The spreadsheet is a work tracker, not the status log, and the failed verification at 15:39 contradicts its label. A draft customer update was prepared at 15:55 and sent at 16:20; customer communication does not define mitigation time. The monitor’s initial latency alert was at 13:02, but the rule starts at confirmed customer impact, not detection of a possible precursor. The 13:11 ticket likewise predates confirmation.\n\nThe postmortem reviewer checked the status history and found no earlier successful test for the affected tenant path. The 16:12 entry records both the cache invalidation and a successful login validation. The 16:28 support note is corroborating evidence, not an earlier recovery event. The remaining work after 16:12 was monitoring, reconciliation, and customer follow-up. The policy has no special adjustment for a signing-key event, tenant count, severity level, or time of day. Use the stated start and first qualifying mitigation, then compare the elapsed time with the four-hour commitment. The incident was not exempt from the commitment.",
