    "state": "Subject: Granite Labs incident notification audit — queue saturation\nFrom: Leila Rahman, service governance\nTo: Operations review group\nDate: 12 November 2025\n\nThis record concerns a single production incident on 10 November. Under the customer operations standard, the first external incident notice must be sent within 45 minutes after the incident is declared. The clock starts at the declaration timestamp recorded by the incident commander. Detection, internal escalation, drafting, and approval are not declaration. The clock stops when the first external notice is sent through the customer notification system; opening or acknowledging that notice is not required. The standard uses elapsed minutes and includes no business-hour pause.\n\nThe monitoring platform first showed elevated queue depth at 06:42 UTC. At 06:51, an analyst opened a diagnostic ticket. The incident commander declared the incident at 07:05, as shown in the signed event log. A separate operations dashboard displays 07:03 beside the incident title, but that field is the time the dashboard card was created, not the declaration. The commander’s signed log is the controlling record for the start.\n\nThe response team began investigating worker saturation at 07:12. At 07:19, a draft notice was saved for review. It said that report generation might be delayed. The draft was edited at 07:27 to include the affected region and then approved at 07:33. None of those actions sent a notice. The notification system audit records the first external send at 07:51. The delivery receipt is timestamped 07:52, and the first customer acknowledgment arrived at 08:06. The standard uses the send event at 07:51.\n\nFor comparison, a maintenance advisory was sent on 9 November at 07:00 for a planned change on 12 November. That advisory was not an incident notice and is included here only because it appears adjacent to the incident in the export. A test alert was sent internally at 07:40 on 10 November to verify the paging connector. It went to staff, not customers, and does not count as the first external notice. A later customer update was sent at 08:14, after the team had more detail; it is not the first notice either.\n\nThe queue began draining at 08:02 after additional workers were assigned. At 08:26, the status page showed normal report generation. Those later recovery times do not affect the notification deadline audit. The notice sent at 07:51 stated that the service team was investigating queue delays and would provide another update. It did not claim the incident was resolved. The standard does not require a complete root cause before initial notification, and no exception was recorded for this incident.\n\nAn earlier draft of this audit incorrectly calculated the period from the 06:42 monitor alert to the 07:51 notice and called it 69 minutes. That is not the policy interval because the incident was not declared at 06:42. Another comment used 07:19, when the notice was drafted, as the stop time. The notification-system audit disproves that. For this review, pair the signed declaration record with the external send record, calculate the elapsed interval, and compare it to 45 minutes. The customer acknowledgment, internal test alert, advisory, and service recovery are supplied to distinguish the relevant events, not to change the measurement rule.",
