    "state": "Subject: Kestrel [No] — avoid name reuse! Different company needed\nActually use Saltmarsh? Already used. Source must have unique names and companies across sources. Use invented company and name.\n\nPost-incident service review: Meridian Loom SaaS export service\nPrepared by Amara Lindqvist, incident review lead\nDate: 3 December 2025\n\nScope and rule\nThis review evaluates the customer-facing restoration objective for a scheduled data-export incident on 29 November. The service schedule defines restoration as the first timestamp when the status page confirms that customers can submit and download a complete export. The restoration objective is six elapsed hours from the incident’s confirmed-impact timestamp. A partial workaround, an internal test, or an estimate that the service is nearly ready does not count. Where an entry is corrected in the signed status history, the corrected timestamp controls. The objective is evaluated against that pair of times only.\n\nIncident timeline, UTC\nAt 01:10, a routine capacity monitor reported a rising storage queue. No customer impact had been confirmed. At 01:38, an analyst opened a ticket after one test export took longer than usual. At 02:04, the on-call lead confirmed that customer export submissions were failing and entered the incident start in the status log. That is the confirmed-impact timestamp. A planning note in the review folder says 02:00, but it refers to the scheduled start of a database maintenance activity, not the customer-impact confirmation.\n\nThe team found that a storage index rebuild had left some export jobs waiting. At 03:12, a restart allowed a subset of customers to submit requests, but completed files were still unavailable. At 04:06, an internal test file downloaded successfully from a test tenant. The public status page remained in investigating state because the test tenant did not represent customer data. At 05:21, the team expanded the export worker pool. Queue depth fell, but several customer exports remained incomplete. These are meaningful mitigation milestones, not restoration under the stated definition.\n\nAt 07:41, the service team believed exports were available and prepared a status-page update. The first draft was saved at 07:44 and said “resolved.” A validation run against a customer-equivalent dataset then failed at 07:52 because one archive was incomplete. The draft was not published. A later status-page entry was prepared at 08:09, but its publication was held while the team checked the final artifact. The signed status history records the first public confirmation that customers could submit and download complete exports at 08:16. A later audit note created at 08:23 repeats that recovery time and links the validation results.\n\nThe incident commander closed the internal response bridge at 08:47 after confirming that queued jobs had drained. Closure of the bridge was not the restoration event: the status page had already recorded complete customer exports at 08:16. A support message at 08:29 asked customers to retry older failed jobs, which was follow-up rather than a later restoration. The next day’s routine capacity report is unrelated to the objective.\n\nReview guidance\nThe deadline is calculated by adding six elapsed hours to confirmed impact at 02:04, producing 08:04. The later confirmation at 08:16 is the qualifying restoration timestamp. The 07:41 belief, unissued draft, and failed 07:52 validation cannot establish that customers could obtain complete exports. The 08:16 status history entry is signed and was not corrected. The schedule contains no exception for overnight incidents, maintenance activity, test-tenant success, or a slow customer queue. Please determine whether restoration met the six-hour objective and record the elapsed duration from the two controlling timestamps. Do not substitute the scheduled maintenance time or internal bridge closure.",
