    "state": "Voicemail transcript and accounts-payable desk notes, Saltmarsh Supply, 14–18 October\n\nMonday, 14 October, 8:06 a.m. Incoming voicemail, auto-transcribed: “Morning, this is Farid Okafor at Saltmarsh Supply. I’m calling about invoice SS-4417, the one for the replacement pump seals. I know the subject line says ‘payment overdue,’ and I know your screen might show our name next to the words ‘please remit.’ I’m not calling to demand payment from Granite Freight or to report that Granite owes us. We bought the seals from Granite, and our accounts-payable team is asking Granite to settle its credit note to us. We paid their original invoice already. They sent the wrong seal material, accepted the return, and issued a credit memo for $1,260. We have not received that credit. Please have someone check whether Granite has applied it against our open balance or is sending the money back. The remaining balance on our side is not $1,260; that number is the credit Granite owes Saltmarsh. I’m in a noisy warehouse, so I may repeat myself: Saltmarsh is waiting on Granite, not the other way around.”\n\nThe voicemail system added its usual metadata: caller ID ending 6041; call duration 1 minute 48 seconds; transcription confidence 92%; category suggestion “supplier invoice overdue.” A clerk’s margin note says the category suggestion is generated from words in the audio and is not a verified statement about who owes whom. The “payment overdue” phrase is spoken while Farid describes the subject line of an old email, not the subject of his own demand. The remaining account balance and the returned goods are separate figures and events.\n\nMonday, 14 October, 8:19 a.m., payment queue snapshot (copied into the desk notes): Saltmarsh has a payable record for Granite Freight’s original invoice GF-8871, gross $4,860, dated 22 August, marked paid 6 September. The payment confirmation is $4,860 exactly. A separate debit memo for $1,260, created 29 August, describes the incorrect pump seals returned to Granite. Granite’s credit memo CM-203, dated 2 September, agrees with the debit memo at $1,260. The system has not matched CM-203 to a refund, replacement shipment, or open payable. Another Granite invoice, GF-8910, for $980 of freight on unrelated pallet racks, is pending approval; it does not erase or change the $1,260 credit. The queue’s top-level “supplier owes us” indicator is blank, because credits awaiting review are not automatically treated as collectible cash. A note beside the indicator reads, “Do not infer direction from vendor/supplier labels; read the underlying transaction.”\n\nMonday, 14 October, 9:03 a.m., internal accounts-payable chat excerpt: “Mina: Does this mean we owe Granite $1,260? The word payable is all over the page.” “Farid: No, that’s our returned-goods credit. We already paid the $4,860 bill.” “Mina: But the supplier field says Granite Freight.” “Farid: Supplier field is for the original invoice. The credit memo is Granite’s adjustment to us.” “Mina: I’m seeing another $980 pending, too.” “Farid: Separate freight invoice, not the seal credit. Please don’t net it without approval.” No payment is authorized in this chat. The chat is included to preserve context, not as an instruction to use its wording instead of the documents.\n\nMonday, 14 October, 10:22 a.m., handwritten-style note typed up by the receiving clerk: “Box came back to dock, yes, but only because we sent the seals back. Seal lot label: PS-17. Not the pump assembly. No issue with the pallet racks on GF-8910. I put the carrier receipt in folder R-8. Farid said accounts needs the credit, not a new delivery. If anybody sees the words ‘owed’ or ‘paid’ in this pile, please look for the subject of the verb. I am not an accountant; I am just trying to keep the rubber seals and steel rack parts in different bins. Someone wrote ‘payment from Saltmarsh’ in red on the outside of the folder. That is probably the original $4,860 payment receipt, not payment of the credit.”\n\nTuesday, 15 October, 7:55 a.m., voicemail follow-up, auto-transcribed: “Farid again. I found the signed return receipt, and it says Granite Freight took the seals back on 30 August. Their credit memo is dated 2 September. I want to be careful not to say they still owe the whole original invoice; the whole invoice was paid. What remains unresolved is only that approved $1,260 credit. Our bookkeeper’s draft note says ‘Saltmarsh owes Granite credit’; that wording is backwards. I corrected it in the copy I sent. The draft was assembled from the supplier ledger, where Granite is the supplier name even on the credit.”\n\nA ledger export attached to the follow-up shows: original invoice GF-8871, debit to Saltmarsh payable, $4,860; payment, credit to payable, $4,860; return authorization RA-55, value $1,260; Granite credit memo CM-203, value $1,260; settlement field, blank. An annotation explains that debit and credit are accounting-column labels in this export. They should not be read as ordinary-language claims that one company owes another. A nearby row for Northstar Packaging, an unrelated vendor, has an unpaid invoice of $1,260. The repeated amount is coincidental and the vendor is not part of this dispute. The export also lists a Saltmarsh customer refund of $126.00 from a different department; that is not a Granite transaction.\n\nTuesday, 15 October, 11:14 a.m., phone note from AP analyst: “Called Granite’s shared line. Agent would not confirm a payment date, but verified CM-203 is still open. Agent said, ‘We owe Saltmarsh the credit, but it may be offset after the October statement is finalized.’ I asked whether they had already sent funds. She said no payment has been released. Do not put ‘paid’ merely because the memo says approved.” The agent’s statement is a recorded confirmation of the direction of the credit, while the final method of settlement remains undecided. The offset is a possibility, not a completed offset, and no offset amount beyond