    "state": "Bluefin Rentals — Employment Agreement, Clause 8 and attached amendments\n\nOriginal Clause 8, signed by both parties: The employee’s normal working location is the Bluefin Rentals service office. The company may approve work from another location for a specified day or period. An employee who wants to work remotely must obtain written approval from their manager before the remote work begins. Approval should identify the relevant date or dates. The employee remains responsible for protecting company records and following the applicable security instructions while working away from the office.\n\nOriginal Clause 8.2: The employee is expected to keep their manager informed of any change that affects the agreed work schedule. A request to change the schedule does not itself change the schedule. The employee should continue to follow the existing arrangement unless the manager confirms a different one in writing. This wording is intended to be clear, although experience suggests that clear wording and confident interpretation are not always close relatives.\n\nAmendment A, effective 1 February: Employees may request a flexible start time by sending a message to their manager at least one working day beforehand. The manager will confirm whether the change is approved. A request made by the deadline is still a request, not automatic approval. If the manager is unavailable, the employee should follow the existing schedule and contact the department coordinator. This amendment does not remove the written-approval requirement for working from another location.\n\nAmendment B, effective 1 April: Employees assigned to the equipment desk may borrow a company laptop for approved work away from the office. The equipment desk records the device number, the employee’s name, and the expected return date. Borrowing a device does not approve remote work. A laptop can travel; permission has not been shown to possess the same mobility.\n\nAmendment C, effective 15 May: Managers may approve a recurring remote-work arrangement for a named employee and specified weekdays. The approval must be in writing and must state its start and end dates. Any day outside the named weekdays remains an office day unless separately approved. If the arrangement expires, employees return to the normal work location unless a renewal is confirmed before the expiry date.\n\nAmendment D, effective 1 June: For an unexpected building closure, the operations director may issue a written notice authorizing remote work for affected employees on the date specified in the notice. The notice does not change any later scheduled office day. Employees should not infer a continuing arrangement from a one-day closure notice. The amendment exists because a closed building and an open-ended interpretation once arrived together and were not an especially productive pair.\n\nAcknowledgment record: Tomasz Petrov received the original clause and amendments A through D by email on 3 June. The record notes that he clicked “acknowledge receipt.” It does not indicate that he requested or received a recurring arrangement. Acknowledging receipt means the documents were delivered; it is not a separate manager’s authorization. The HR administrator entered the acknowledgment in the file on 4 June.\n\nManager email, 6 June, 3:12 p.m.: “Tomasz, I saw your note asking whether you could work from home on Thursday, 13 June. I have not approved that day. Please plan to work from the service office unless I send written approval. I’ll check staffing before confirming.”\n\nTomasz Petrov, 6 June, 3:20 p.m.: “Understood. I have not made arrangements. I’ll wait for your confirmation.”\n\nManager email, 7 June, 10:05 a.m.: “Approved: you may work remotely on Thursday, 13 June only. This does not alter your normal schedule for any other day.”\n\nHR file note, 7 June, 10:22 a.m.: The manager’s email is saved under the employee’s file as written approval for the date stated. No recurring schedule was entered. Tomasz’s normal work location remains the service office for all other dates unless a later written approval is issued.",
