ses and I would prefer not to interpret it through vibes.\n\n8:29 — People Ops, Mina: Ordinary commuting between home and the assigned office is not reimbursed. Approved work travel between work sites may be claimed with a receipt. If your manager asks you to travel to another site for a meeting, submit the route, purpose, and receipt in the expenses portal. A journey from home to your regular office remains a commute even if the train is unpleasant or the weather has developed a personality.\n\n8:35 — Rosa Kowalski: I only mean the daily trip to my usual office. I haven’t traveled to another site.\n\n8:37 — People Ops, Mina: Then that fare is not claimable under the handbook. It is still possible to ask your manager about a separate arrangement, but no such arrangement appears in the current policy record.\n\n8:46 — Rosa Kowalski: One more thing. My access card stopped opening the supply cabinet yesterday. Does the handbook say it is a disciplinary matter?\n\n8:51 — People Ops, Mina: No. A card failure should be reported to facilities so they can check the access list or replace the card. The handbook says lost cards should be reported promptly; it does not say that a technical failure is misconduct. Please include the cabinet location and the approximate time it failed. Do not send the card number in this chat.\n\n8:56 — Rosa Kowalski: It failed at 4:20 p.m. at the west supply cabinet. I still have the card.\n\n9:01 — People Ops, Mina: I have opened a facilities ticket with that description. Facilities will contact you. This is a ticket, not a diagnosis, a reprimand, or a small ceremony honoring the cabinet’s independence.\n\n9:07 — Rosa Kowalski: Understood. I’ll submit the course request and wait before registering.\n\n9:09 — People Ops, Mina: That is the right order. The handbook is available in the staff portal under Policies. The version marked 12 June is current. Old screenshots may still circulate, because deleting an old screenshot apparently requires a co