    "state": "Harbor Supply — Internal People Policy: Learning, Attendance, and Office Purchases\n\nPurpose. This policy records the basic steps employees follow when requesting training, changing their work location, or buying routine supplies. It applies to all staff unless a signed role-specific agreement says otherwise. The policy is written in plain language, an ambitious description of any document containing more than one heading.\n\nLearning support. Employees may request financial support for training that relates to their current duties or an agreed development goal. The request should be made before enrollment and include the provider, course dates, fee, and a short explanation of relevance. The line manager reviews the request. The learning coordinator records an approved registration. Employees should wait for confirmation before paying or reserving a place themselves. A course appearing on someone’s wish list, calendar, or enthusiastic team message is not the same as an approved enrollment.\n\nThe annual learning limit is set in each employee’s portal record. The limit indicates the maximum available for approved learning, not an automatic entitlement to spend the full amount. Fees above the limit require separate written approval from the department head. Books or materials may be included only when they are listed in the approval. A manager may approve a lower amount than requested. If a course is cancelled, the employee should notify the coordinator so the record can be updated; the budget does not transform into a personal cash award simply because an event disappeared.\n\nAttendance and location. An employee’s assigned workplace is listed in their employment record. A manager may approve a different location for specific dates or as a written recurring arrangement. A one-day approval covers only that stated day. Employees who need a recurring arrangement should request dates and obtain written confirmation before relying on it. Having a laptop, a clear calendar, or a strong preference for a quieter chair does not establish permission to work elsewhere.\n\nEmployees should notify their manager if illness or an unexpected delay affects attendance. A notification helps the team plan but does not itself authorize paid leave. Leave is recorded through the appropriate HR process. Managers should respond as soon as practical. If no response has arrived, employees should not represent the request as approved. The process may seem needlessly formal until somebody tries to reconcile three different versions of where a person was expected to be.\n\nPurchases. Office supplies should normally be ordered through the approved purchasing account. A manager must approve any non-routine purchase before an order is placed. Routine low-cost items may be ordered by a designated office coordinator within the department’s monthly allocation. Employees who buy items personally without prior approval may submit the receipt for review, but reimbursement is not guaranteed. The word “receipt” documents a transaction; it does not retroactively create authorization.\n\nEquipment borrowed for a work task should be logged with the equipment coordinator and returned by the agreed date. Personal equipment must not be connected to protected company systems without authorization from IT. A staff member who finds an unlabelled device should report it rather than quietly adopting it as a perk of being observant.\n\nExpenses. Claims for approved business travel must include the date, business purpose, route, and itemized receipt. Ordinary travel between an employee’s home and assigned workplace is not reimbursed. A manager may approve travel to another company site or an external work event. The approval should be retained with the claim. A colleague’s suggestion that a route is “basically work travel” has no special status in the expense system.\n\nRecords and privacy. Employees should use the appropriate restricted channel for confidential medical or personal information. General team chat is visible to people who do not need that information. HR keeps only records needed to administer employment and internal processes. Employees may ask HR to correct a factual error in their record. A correction request does not erase a valid historical entry; it adds an appropriate explanation or updated information.\n\nQuestions. Employees may ask their manager or People Operations to clarify this policy. Written departmental updates take precedence over older copies on shared drives. A forwarded excerpt without its date may be useful for locating a topic, but it is not the reliable source of the current rule.",
