    "state": "Northwind Health — Scheduling and payment rules\nRevised: September 3\n\nPatients are asked to arrive fifteen minutes before a scheduled visit. A late arrival does not automatically become a cancellation. Staff may mark a visit missed only after the patient has failed to check in and the clinician has released the room. If a clinician is delayed, the original booking remains active unless the patient agrees to a different time. A text offering an alternate slot is not, by itself, a confirmed change.\n\nA prepayment is required for procedures listed in the procedure catalogue. Routine consultations do not require advance payment. When a prepayment is collected, it is applied to the final invoice; it is not an additional charge. A payment authorization that is declined does not count as money received. Staff must not describe an authorization attempt as a completed payment.\n\nRescheduling requests must be recorded with the date and time received. The office allows one complimentary change when the request arrives at least two business days before the visit. Weekends and listed clinic closure days are not business days. The free-change allowance concerns the number and timing of changes, not a special type of medical service. Requests closer to the visit may be reviewed by a supervisor, who can waive a fee but cannot claim that the patient already agreed to a new slot.\n\nA note from a patient saying “I can probably make Thursday if my ride is available” is not a confirmed acceptance. Staff should seek an unambiguous answer. Messages copied into a file must be read in context; a quoted question from a receptionist does not establish that the patient asked for a particular service. The current catalogue contains imaging, consultations, and procedures. It does not list a “priority appointment” product or a “refund appointment.” If a ticket does not fit any named service or rule category presented in its available choices, use the general unmatched-case route rather than inventing a category.\n\nComplaints about staff conduct should be routed to the office manager, while billing disputes go to the accounts desk. An angry tone alone does not determine the destination. Staff must identify what the person actually wants before routing a case. The policy is not permission to infer a treatment, payment, or booking from a tentative remark. For conflicting records, preserve the discrepancy and request human review rather than silently selecting whichever entry appears latest.",
